window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-phy-current","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Current Electricity · Practice","chapter":"Current Electricity","subject":"Physics","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":1,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform current density $\\vec{j} = (3\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j}) \\times 10^{6}$ A/m$^2$ exists in a region. What is the current through a small flat area whose area vector is $\\Delta \\vec{S} = (2\\hat{i} + \\hat{k}) \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$?","image":"","options":["6.0 A","11.2 A","10 A","14 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\vec{j} = (3, 4, 0) \\times 10^{6}$ A/m$^2$ and $\\Delta \\vec{S} = (2, 0, 1) \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$.\nFormula: $I = \\vec{j} \\cdot \\Delta \\vec{S}$.\nSubstituting: $I = (3 \\times 2 + 4 \\times 0 + 0 \\times 1) \\times 10^{6} \\times 10^{-6}$.\nSo I = 6.0 A.\nMultiplying the magnitudes ($5 \\times 10^6 \\times \\sqrt{5} \\times 10^{-6}$) gives 11.2 A. That is wrong because it ignores the dot product.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":2,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In a solid metallic conductor carrying a current, which particles carry the current?","image":"","options":["Free electrons moving against a background of fixed positive ions","Positive ions moving while the electrons stay fixed","Both positive ions and electrons moving in opposite directions","Nuclei of the metal atoms vibrating about their mean positions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In a solid conductor the atoms are tightly bound to one another, so the positive ions stay fixed.\nIn metals, some electrons are practically free to move through the bulk material.\nSo the current is carried only by these free electrons, moving against the fixed positive ions.\nBoth positive and negative charges move only in conductors such as electrolytic solutions, not in solid metals.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":3,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Four wires meet at a junction P in a circuit carrying steady currents. Wire 1 brings 3 A into P and wire 2 brings 5 A into P. Wire 3 carries 2 A away from P. What is the current in wire 4, which is directed away from P?","image":"","options":["6 A","10 A","4 A","0 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: entering currents are 3 A and 5 A. Leaving currents are 2 A and $I_4$.\nFormula (junction rule): sum of currents entering = sum of currents leaving.\nSubstituting: $3 + 5 = 2 + I_4$.\nSo $I_4$ = 6 A, directed away from P.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":4,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Two cells are connected in series so that the current leaves each cell from its positive electrode. One cell has emf 1.5 V and internal resistance 0.5 $\\Omega$. The other has emf 2.0 V and internal resistance 0.3 $\\Omega$. The combination is connected across an external resistor of 3.2 $\\Omega$. What is the current in the circuit?","image":"","options":["0.70 A","0.875 A","0.125 A","1.09 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\varepsilon_1$ = 1.5 V, $r_1$ = 0.5 $\\Omega$, $\\varepsilon_2$ = 2.0 V, $r_2$ = 0.3 $\\Omega$, R = 3.2 $\\Omega$, with the cells aiding each other.\nFormula: $\\varepsilon_{eq} = \\varepsilon_1 + \\varepsilon_2$, $r_{eq} = r_1 + r_2$, and $I = \\frac{\\varepsilon_{eq}}{R + r_{eq}}$.\nSubstituting: $\\varepsilon_{eq}$ = 3.5 V and $r_{eq}$ = 0.8 $\\Omega$, so I = 3.5/(3.2 + 0.8) = 3.5/4.\nSo I = 0.875 A. Ignoring the internal resistances would wrongly give 1.09 A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":5,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"For a metallic conductor with n free electrons per unit volume, each of charge magnitude e and mass m, relaxation time $\\tau$, placed in an electric field E, match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(P) Conductivity $\\sigma$\n(Q) Mobility $\\mu$\n(R) Drift speed $v_d$\n(S) Magnitude of current density $j$\nList-II\n(1) $\\frac{e\\tau}{m}$\n(2) $nev_d$\n(3) $\\frac{ne^2\\tau}{m}$\n(4) $\\frac{eE\\tau}{m}$","image":"","options":["P-1, Q-3, R-4, S-2","P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2","P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2","P-3, Q-1, R-2, S-4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(R) Averaging the velocity over all electrons gives $v_d = eE\\tau/m$, so R-4.\n(Q) Mobility is $\\mu = v_d/E = e\\tau/m$, so Q-1.\n(S) The charge crossing unit area per second is $j = nev_d$, so S-2.\n(P) Substituting $v_d$ gives $j = (ne^2\\tau/m)E$. Comparing with $j = \\sigma E$ shows $\\sigma = ne^2\\tau/m$, so P-3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":6,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"The current across a cross-section of a wire, measured in the forward direction, varies with time as $I(t) = 4 - 2t$ (I in amperes, t in seconds). What is the average current in the forward direction over the interval t = 0 to t = 3 s?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{5}{3}$ A","2 A","1 A","$\\frac{4}{3}$ A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $I(t) = 4 - 2t$, from t = 0 to t = 3 s.\nFormula: since $I = \\frac{dQ}{dt}$, the net forward charge is $\\Delta Q = \\int_0^3 I\\,dt$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta Q = [4t - t^2]_0^3 = 12 - 9 = 3$ C.\nThe current reverses at t = 2 s, so the backward charge (1 C) cancels part of the forward charge (4 C).\nSo the average current is $\\frac{3}{3} = 1$ A. Adding the magnitudes instead gives the wrong value $\\frac{5}{3}$ A.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":7,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A meter bridge uses a uniform wire 100 cm long. A known resistance of 6 Ω is placed in the left gap and an unknown resistance X in the right gap. The null point is found at 40 cm from the left end of the wire. Since the wire is uniform, the resistance of each wire segment is proportional to its length. What is X?","image":"","options":["4 Ω","9 Ω","6 Ω","15 Ω"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: R = 6 Ω, left segment $l$ = 40 cm, right segment $100 - l$ = 60 cm. \nFormula: the two wire segments form two arms of a Wheatstone bridge. For a uniform wire $R \\propto l$, so $\\frac{R}{X} = \\frac{l}{100 - l}$. \nSubstituting: $X = 6 \\times \\frac{60}{40}$. \nSo X = 9 Ω. Taking the ratio upside down would give the wrong value of 4 Ω. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":8,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The algebraic sum of changes in potential around any closed loop of resistors and cells is zero.\nReason (R): Electric potential depends only on the location of a point, so returning to the starting point gives zero total change.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. It is Kirchhoff's loop rule.\nR is true. Potential has a definite value at each point.\nGoing around a closed loop brings you back to the same point, so the net change in potential must be zero.\nR is therefore exactly the reason the loop rule holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":9,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about Kirchhoff's rules are correct?\n(i) The junction rule applies equally to a single point on a wire, not only to a junction of several wires.\n(ii) Bending or reorienting a wire at a junction can make the junction rule invalid.\n(iii) The junction rule relies on there being no accumulation of charge at any point when currents are steady.\n(iv) The loop rule can be applied to a closed loop that contains cells as well as resistors.\n(v) In any network, every possible closed loop gives a new independent equation.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. NCERT notes that the rule also holds at a point on a line.\n(ii) Incorrect. The rule is charge conservation, which is unaffected by the geometry of the wire.\n(iii) Correct. Steady currents mean no charge build-up anywhere.\n(iv) Correct. The loop rule applies to loops containing resistors and cells.\n(v) Incorrect. In Example 3.6, the remaining loops gave no additional independent equations.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":10,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the internal resistance of cells is correct according to NCERT?","image":"","options":["All cells of the same emf have the same internal resistance","The internal resistance of a dry cell is much higher than that of common electrolytic cells","A dry cell has practically zero internal resistance","Internal resistance is the resistance of the external connecting wires of the cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Internal resistance is the finite resistance of the electrolyte through which current flows inside the cell.\nNCERT notes that its value varies from cell to cell. This rules out option (1).\nNCERT also states that the internal resistance of dry cells is much higher than that of common electrolytic cells. So option (2) is correct and option (3) is false.\nOption (4) is wrong because internal resistance lies inside the cell, not in the external wires.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":11,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (physical quantity) with List-II (dimensional formula).\nList-I:\n(A) Resistivity\n(B) Electrical conductivity\n(C) Current density\n(D) Resistance\nList-II:\n(P) $[M^{-1} L^{-3} T^{3} A^{2}]$\n(Q) $[M L^{2} T^{-3} A^{-2}]$\n(R) $[L^{-2} A]$\n(S) $[M L^{3} T^{-3} A^{-2}]$","image":"","options":["A-S, B-P, C-R, D-Q","A-Q, B-P, C-R, D-S","A-S, B-R, C-P, D-Q","A-P, B-S, C-R, D-Q"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(D) Resistance: $R = V/I$, with $[V] = [ML^2T^{-3}A^{-1}]$. So $[R] = [ML^2T^{-3}A^{-2}]$, which is Q.\n(A) Resistivity: $\\rho = RA/l$. This multiplies R by length, giving $[ML^3T^{-3}A^{-2}]$, which is S.\n(B) Conductivity: $\\sigma = 1/\\rho$, giving $[M^{-1}L^{-3}T^3A^2]$, which is P.\n(C) Current density: $j = I/A$, giving $[L^{-2}A]$, which is R.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":12,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following do NOT obey Ohm's law (in the sense of a linear, unique, sign-independent V–I relation)?\n(i) A semiconductor diode\n(ii) A copper wire kept at constant temperature and moderate field\n(iii) Gallium arsenide (GaAs)\n(iv) A rectifier\n(v) A silver wire at constant temperature and moderate field","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Diode: non-ohmic. Its V–I relation depends on the sign of V.\n(ii) Copper wire at fixed temperature: ohmic.\n(iii) GaAs: non-ohmic. More than one V can give the same I.\n(iv) Rectifier: non-ohmic. It is both non-linear and sign-dependent.\n(v) Silver wire at moderate field: ohmic.\nSo three items deviate from Ohm's law.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":13,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Kirchhoff's junction rule states that at any junction the total current entering equals the total current leaving. On which principle is this rule based?","image":"","options":["Conservation of energy","Conservation of charge, since charge does not accumulate at a junction when currents are steady","Conservation of linear momentum of the electrons","Ohm's law applied to the wires meeting at the junction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Current is the rate at which charge flows.\nWhen currents are steady, no charge piles up at a junction or at any point on a line.\nSo the rate at which charge flows in must equal the rate at which it flows out. This is conservation of charge.\nConservation of energy is the basis of the loop rule, not the junction rule.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":14,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (behaviour) with List-II (example).\nList-I:\n(A) More than one value of V for the same current I\n(B) Relation between V and I depends on the sign of V\n(C) V ceases to be proportional to I at large currents\n(D) Resistivity nearly independent of temperature\nList-II:\n(p) GaAs\n(q) Diode\n(r) Manganin\n(s) A good conductor whose V–I curve departs from a straight line","image":"","options":["A-p, B-q, C-s, D-r","A-q, B-p, C-s, D-r","A-p, B-q, C-r, D-s","A-s, B-q, C-p, D-r"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) A non-unique V–I relation is shown by GaAs. Match: p.\n(B) Reversing V does not reverse the current with the same magnitude in a diode. Match: q.\n(C) The solid curve for a good conductor in Fig. 3.5 bends away from the linear Ohm's law line. Match: s.\n(D) Manganin, like nichrome and constantan, has a resistivity that varies very little with temperature. Match: r.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":15,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (medium) with List-II (mobile charge carriers / behaviour).\nList-I:\n(A) Metal\n(B) Ionised gas\n(C) Electrolyte\n(D) Insulator such as plastic\nList-II:\n(I) Positive and negative ions\n(II) Electrons, against fixed positive ions\n(III) Electrons and positive ions\n(IV) Electrons stay bound and do not accelerate in an applied field","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Metal: free electrons move while the positive ions stay fixed, so A-II.\n(B) Ionised gas: both electrons and positive ions are mobile, so B-III.\n(C) Electrolyte: positive and negative ions carry the charge, so C-I.\n(D) Insulator: the electrons remain bound and do not accelerate in a field, so D-IV.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":16,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In an ionised gas, which particles act as the mobile charge carriers that give rise to conductivity?","image":"","options":["Only electrons","Only positive ions","Electrons and positively charged ions","Positive and negative ions only, with no free electrons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: the type of mobile charge carrier depends on the medium.\nIn metals the carriers are electrons. In an ionised gas they are electrons and positively charged ions. In an electrolyte they can be both positive and negative ions.\nOption (4) describes an electrolyte, not an ionised gas. An ionised gas contains free electrons knocked out of its atoms.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":17,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A wire 2.0 m long has a cross-sectional area of $1.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$ and carries a steady current of 1.6 A. The free electron density is $8.0 \\times 10^{28}$ m$^{-3}$ and $e = 1.6\\times10^{-19}$ C. How long does an electron take to drift from one end of the wire to the other?","image":"","options":["$1.6 \\times 10^{4}$ s","$1.6 \\times 10^{3}$ s","$4.0 \\times 10^{3}$ s","$6.4 \\times 10^{4}$ s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: l = 2.0 m, $A = 1.0\\times10^{-6}$ m$^2$, I = 1.6 A, $n = 8.0\\times10^{28}$ m$^{-3}$.\nFormula: $v_d = \\frac{I}{neA}$ and $t = \\frac{l}{v_d}$.\nSubstituting: $v_d = \\frac{1.6}{8.0\\times10^{28}\\times1.6\\times10^{-19}\\times10^{-6}} = 1.25\\times10^{-4}$ m/s.\nThen $t = \\frac{2.0}{1.25\\times10^{-4}} = 1.6\\times10^4$ s, which is about 4.4 hours.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":18,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"What are the dimensions of electrical resistivity $\\rho$?","image":"","options":["$[M L^3 T^{-3} A^{-2}]$","$[M L^2 T^{-3} A^{-2}]$","$[M^{-1} L^{-3} T^3 A^2]$","$[M L^3 T^{-2} A^{-2}]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Resistance has dimensions $[M L^2 T^{-3} A^{-2}]$, since $R = \\frac{V}{I}$.\nFrom $R = \\frac{\\rho l}{A}$, we get $\\rho = \\frac{RA}{l}$. This multiplies the dimensions of R by $L^2 / L = L$.\nSo $\\rho = [M L^3 T^{-3} A^{-2}]$, and its SI unit is $\\Omega$ m.\nOption (2) is the dimension of resistance itself. Option (3) is the dimension of conductivity.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":19,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a balanced Wheatstone bridge, doubling the emf of the cell in the battery arm does not disturb the balance.\nReason (R): The balance condition involves only the four resistances of the bridge arms. It does not depend on the emf of the source.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The balance condition $\\frac{R_2}{R_1} = \\frac{R_4}{R_3}$ comes from the loop rule with $I_g = 0$. Only resistance ratios appear in it. \nR is therefore true. \nDoubling the emf doubles every current in the arms. However, the potentials at the two galvanometer ends stay equal, so $I_g$ remains zero. A is true. \nA holds precisely because the condition contains no emf, so R explains A. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":20,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Doubling the length of a uniform conductor, keeping its cross-section fixed, doubles its resistance.\n(ii) Halving the cross-sectional area of a conductor, keeping its length fixed, halves its resistance.\n(iii) The resistivity of a material depends on temperature.\n(iv) In a conductor obeying Ohm's law, the current density vector $\\vec{j}$ is parallel to the electric field $\\vec{E}$, with $\\vec{j} = \\sigma \\vec{E}$.\n(v) Any device for which we can calculate $R = \\frac{V}{I}$ automatically obeys Ohm's law.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: two identical slabs placed end to end give 2V for the same current I, so $R \\propto l$.\n(ii) Incorrect: halving the area doubles the resistance, since $R \\propto \\frac{1}{A}$.\n(iii) Correct: $\\rho$ depends on temperature; for metals, $\\rho_T = \\rho_0[1 + \\alpha(T - T_0)]$.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT writes $\\vec{j} = \\sigma \\vec{E}$, with $\\vec{j}$ along $\\vec{E}$.\n(v) Incorrect: $V = IR$ only defines R. Ohm's law requires R to be independent of V, and a diode, for example, violates this.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":21,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A cell of emf 6.0 V and internal resistance $0.5\\ \\Omega$ is connected across an external resistor of $2.5\\ \\Omega$. What is the potential difference across the terminals of the cell?","image":"","options":["5.0 V","6.0 V","1.0 V","4.8 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\varepsilon$ = 6.0 V, r = 0.5 Ω, R = 2.5 Ω\nFormula: $I = \\frac{\\varepsilon}{R+r}$ and terminal voltage $V = \\varepsilon - Ir = IR$\nSubstituting: I = 6.0/3.0 = 2.0 A, so V = 2.0 × 2.5 = 5.0 V\nThe value 6.0 V would be the terminal voltage only on open circuit. The value 1.0 V is just the drop Ir across the internal resistance.\nSo the terminal voltage = 5.0 V\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":22,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A cell of emf 6.0 V and internal resistance 0.5 $\\Omega$ is connected across an external resistor of 5.5 $\\Omega$. What current flows in the circuit?","image":"","options":["1.09 A","12 A","1.0 A","0.92 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\varepsilon = 6.0$ V, r = 0.5 $\\Omega$, R = 5.5 $\\Omega$.\nFormula: $I = \\frac{\\varepsilon}{R + r}$\nSubstituting: $I = \\frac{6.0}{5.5 + 0.5} = \\frac{6.0}{6.0}$\nSo I = 1.0 A.\nOption (1) ignores the internal resistance (6/5.5). Option (2) is the short-circuit current $\\varepsilon/r$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":23,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A Wheatstone bridge ABCD has arms AB = 10 Ω, BC = 20 Ω, AD = 20 Ω and DC = 10 Ω. A galvanometer of resistance 10 Ω is connected between B and D. A 6 V source of negligible internal resistance keeps A at 6 V and C at 0 V. What current flows through the galvanometer?","image":"","options":["85.7 mA","0 mA","42.9 mA","120 mA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{10}{20} \\neq \\frac{20}{10}$, so the bridge is unbalanced. \nFormula: apply the junction rule at B and D, with $V_A = 6$ V and $V_C = 0$. \nAt B: $\\frac{6 - V_B}{10} = \\frac{V_B}{20} + \\frac{V_B - V_D}{10}$, which gives $5V_B - 2V_D = 12$. \nAt D: $\\frac{6 - V_D}{20} = \\frac{V_D}{10} + \\frac{V_D - V_B}{10}$, which gives $5V_D - 2V_B = 6$. \nSubstituting: solving gives $V_B = \\frac{24}{7}$ V and $V_D = \\frac{18}{7}$ V. \nSo $I_g = \\frac{6/7}{10}$ A = 85.7 mA, flowing from B to D. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":24,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A copper wire 2.0 m long has a cross-sectional area of $1.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$ and carries a steady current of 2.0 A. The free electron density of copper is $8.5 \\times 10^{28}$ m$^{-3}$ and $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C. How long does an electron take to drift from one end of the wire to the other?","image":"","options":["$1.36 \\times 10^{4}$ s","$1.36 \\times 10^{3}$ s","$6.8 \\times 10^{3}$ s","$2.72 \\times 10^{4}$ s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: l = 2.0 m, A = $1.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$, I = 2.0 A, n = $8.5 \\times 10^{28}$ m$^{-3}$.\nFormula: $v_d = \\frac{I}{neA}$ and $t = \\frac{l}{v_d} = \\frac{neAl}{I}$\nSubstituting: $t = \\frac{8.5 \\times 10^{28} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 1.0 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 2.0}{2.0}$\nSo t = $1.36 \\times 10^{4}$ s, which is about 3.8 hours.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":25,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A cell of emf 12 V and internal resistance 2 $\\Omega$ is connected across an external resistor of 4 $\\Omega$. At what rate is the chemical energy of the cell being converted into electrical energy?","image":"","options":["16 W","8 W","36 W","24 W"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $\\varepsilon = 12$ V, $r = 2\\ \\Omega$, $R = 4\\ \\Omega$.\nCurrent: $I = \\frac{\\varepsilon}{R + r} = \\frac{12}{6} = 2$ A.\nPower in R: $I^2R = 16$ W. Power lost inside the cell: $I^2r = 8$ W.\nThe cell's chemical energy supplies both, so total power = $\\varepsilon I = 12 \\times 2$ = 24 W.\nOption (1), 16 W, counts only the external resistor and ignores the internal loss.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":26,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Four identical cells, each of emf $\\varepsilon$ and internal resistance $r$, are connected in parallel with all positive terminals joined together and all negative terminals joined together. The equivalent emf and equivalent internal resistance of the combination are, respectively:","image":"","options":["$\\varepsilon$ and $\\frac{r}{4}$","$4\\varepsilon$ and $\\frac{r}{4}$","$\\varepsilon$ and $4r$","$4\\varepsilon$ and $4r$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For cells in parallel, NCERT gives $\\frac{1}{r_{eq}} = \\sum \\frac{1}{r_i}$ and $\\frac{\\varepsilon_{eq}}{r_{eq}} = \\sum \\frac{\\varepsilon_i}{r_i}$.\nInternal resistance: $\\frac{1}{r_{eq}} = \\frac{4}{r}$, so $r_{eq} = \\frac{r}{4}$.\nEmf: $\\frac{\\varepsilon_{eq}}{r/4} = \\frac{4\\varepsilon}{r}$, so $\\varepsilon_{eq} = \\varepsilon$.\nThe tempting choice $4\\varepsilon$ is wrong. Emfs add only when the cells are in series. Identical cells in parallel keep the same emf and simply share the current.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":27,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Metals are good conductors because their resistivity is low. According to NCERT, the typical range of resistivity of metals is:","image":"","options":["$10^{-8}\\ \\Omega$ m to $10^{-6}\\ \\Omega$ m","$10^{-2}\\ \\Omega$ m to $10^{2}\\ \\Omega$ m","$10^{10}\\ \\Omega$ m to $10^{14}\\ \\Omega$ m","$10^{-14}\\ \\Omega$ m to $10^{-12}\\ \\Omega$ m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that metals have low resistivities, in the range $10^{-8}\\ \\Omega$ m to $10^{-6}\\ \\Omega$ m.\nInsulators such as rubber and plastics have resistivities $10^{18}$ times greater or more, so option (3) is closer to the insulator side.\nSemiconductors lie in between.\nThe range in option (4) is far too small for any real material.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":28,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform wire of resistance 10 $\\Omega$ is stretched uniformly until its length becomes three times the original length. Its volume and resistivity stay the same. What is its new resistance?","image":"","options":["30 $\\Omega$","90 $\\Omega$","3.3 $\\Omega$","270 $\\Omega$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: R = 10 $\\Omega$, new length $l' = 3l$, volume constant.\nConstant volume means $Al = A'l'$, so $A' = \\frac{A}{3}$.\nFormula: $R' = \\frac{\\rho l'}{A'} = \\rho \\frac{3l}{A/3} = 9R$. In general, $R \\propto l^2$ at constant volume.\nSubstituting: $R' = 9 \\times 10 = 90$ $\\Omega$.\nOption (1) wrongly ignores the reduction in area.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":29,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a cell sends a current through an external resistor, the potential difference between its terminals is less than its emf.\nReason (R): The current flowing through the electrolyte meets the internal resistance r of the cell. This reduces the terminal potential difference to $\\varepsilon - Ir$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the emf equals the terminal potential difference only in open circuit, when no current flows.\nR is true: the electrolyte has a finite internal resistance r. When a current I flows through the cell from N to P, the terminal voltage becomes $V = \\varepsilon - Ir$.\nSince $Ir > 0$ whenever $I \\neq 0$, we get $V < \\varepsilon$. R therefore directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":30,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a series combination of two cells, reversing the connections of one cell changes the equivalent emf but leaves the equivalent internal resistance unchanged.\nReason (R): In a series combination, the internal resistances add up whichever electrode the current leaves a cell from, but the emf of a cell enters with a negative sign if the current leaves it from its negative electrode.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) A is true. Reversing one cell changes $\\varepsilon_{eq}$ from $\\varepsilon_1 + \\varepsilon_2$ to $\\varepsilon_1 - \\varepsilon_2$, while $r_{eq}$ stays $r_1 + r_2$.\n(ii) R is true. This is exactly the NCERT sign rule for series cells, and the resistances lie in the current's path either way.\n(iii) R states precisely why the emf changes but the resistance does not, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":31,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The electromotive force of a cell is not a force.\nReason (R): The emf of a cell is the potential difference between its positive and negative electrodes when no current flows through the cell.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that emf is actually a potential difference and not a force. The name survives only for historical reasons.\nR is true. From $\\varepsilon = V_+ + V_-$, emf is the potential difference between P and N in open circuit.\nR explains A. Emf is a potential difference, measured in volts (work per unit charge), so it cannot be a force.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":32,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A resistor R is connected across the terminals of an electrolytic cell, and heat is produced in R. According to NCERT, where does the energy dissipated in R come from?","image":"","options":["Kinetic energy stored in the free electrons of the wire","Chemical energy of the cell","Thermal energy drawn from the surroundings","Rest-mass energy of the electrons that are used up in the resistor"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A steady current needs an external source, and that source must supply the power dissipated.\nIn a cell, the chemical energy of the electrolyte supplies this power for as long as it can.\nOption (1) fails: electrons drift with a steady average velocity, so they store no growing kinetic energy.\nOption (4) fails: electrons are not consumed in the resistor.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":33,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform metal wire has length 2.0 m and cross-sectional area $1.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$. The resistivity of the metal is $1.7 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\Omega$ m. What is the resistance of the wire?","image":"","options":["0.034 Ω","0.017 Ω","0.068 Ω","0.0085 Ω"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $l = 2.0$ m, $A = 1.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$, $\\rho = 1.7 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\Omega$ m.\nFormula: $R = \\frac{\\rho l}{A}$.\nSubstituting: $R = \\frac{1.7 \\times 10^{-8} \\times 2.0}{1.0 \\times 10^{-6}} = 3.4 \\times 10^{-2}\\ \\Omega$.\nSo R = 0.034 Ω. Using half the length instead of the full length would give the tempting wrong value 0.017 Ω.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":34,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a metal wire is stretched to twice its length at constant temperature, its resistance changes but its resistivity stays the same.\nReason (R): Resistivity depends on the material of the conductor (and on temperature), but not on its dimensions.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Stretching changes l and A, so $R = \\frac{\\rho l}{A}$ changes; in fact it becomes four times larger. The material and temperature are unchanged, so $\\rho$ stays the same.\nR is true. NCERT says $\\rho$ depends on the material but not on the dimensions; it does depend on temperature.\nR directly explains why $\\rho$ is unchanged in A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":35,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The electromotive force of a cell is measured in volts, even though it is called a 'force'.\nReason (R): The emf of a cell is the potential difference between its positive and negative electrodes when no current is drawn from it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: emf is not a force. The name survives only for historical reasons, and its unit is the volt.\nR is true: in open circuit, $\\varepsilon = V_+ + V_-$, which is the potential difference between P and N.\nSince emf is a potential difference, it must be measured in volts. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":36,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (material) with List-II (electrical behaviour).\nList-I:\n(A) Nichrome\n(B) Copper\n(C) A typical semiconductor\n(D) GaAs\nList-II:\n(P) Resistivity decreases as temperature rises\n(Q) More than one value of V for the same current I\n(R) Very weak dependence of resistivity on temperature; used in standard resistors\n(S) Resistivity rises nearly linearly with temperature near room temperature, with positive $\\alpha$\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-R, B-S, C-P, D-Q","A-S, B-R, C-P, D-Q","A-R, B-S, C-Q, D-P","A-P, B-S, C-R, D-Q"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Nichrome has resistivity that barely changes with temperature, which suits standard resistors: R.\n(B) Copper is a metal with positive $\\alpha$, and its graph is nearly linear near room temperature: S.\n(C) In a semiconductor, n increases with temperature, so $\\rho$ falls: P.\n(D) GaAs shows a non-unique V–I relation: Q.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":37,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Wire-bound standard resistors are usually made from alloys such as manganin or constantan rather than from copper.\nReason (R): The resistivity of these alloys depends only very weakly on temperature.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that nichrome, manganin and constantan are widely used in wire-bound standard resistors.\nR is true. These alloys show a very weak variation of resistivity with temperature, unlike copper, whose resistivity rises noticeably as it gets hotter.\nR explains A. A standard resistor must keep its value as its temperature changes, so a material with a nearly constant resistivity is chosen.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":38,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In a platinum resistance thermometer, the platinum wire has a resistance of $4.0\\ \\Omega$ at the ice point and $4.4\\ \\Omega$ at the steam point. When placed in a furnace, its resistance becomes $5.1\\ \\Omega$. Assuming a linear variation of resistance with temperature, the temperature of the furnace is:","image":"","options":["$175^\\circ$C","$275^\\circ$C","$27.5^\\circ$C","$375^\\circ$C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $R_0 = 4.0\\ \\Omega$, $R_{100} = 4.4\\ \\Omega$, $R_t = 5.1\\ \\Omega$.\nFormula: from $R_t = R_0(1+\\alpha t)$, we get $t = \\frac{R_t - R_0}{R_{100}-R_0}\\times 100$.\nSubstituting: $t = \\frac{1.1}{0.4}\\times100$.\nSo $t = 275^\\circ$C.\nOption (1) results from wrongly using $R_{100}$ in place of $R_0$ in the numerator.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":39,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (conductor or device) with List-II (observed behaviour).\nList-I:\n(P) GaAs\n(Q) Junction diode\n(R) Silver wire at fixed temperature in a moderate field\n(S) Pure germanium in an extremely strong electric field\nList-II:\n(1) More than one value of V for the same current I\n(2) Current magnitude for $+V$ differs from that for $-V$\n(3) Straight-line I-V graph through the origin\n(4) Departure from Ohm's law because the field is too strong","image":"","options":["P-2, Q-1, R-3, S-4","P-1, Q-2, R-4, S-3","P-1, Q-2, R-3, S-4","P-4, Q-2, R-3, S-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(P) GaAs shows a non-unique V-I relation, so P-1.\n(Q) A diode's V-I relation depends on the sign of V, so Q-2.\n(R) Homogeneous conductors such as silver obey Ohm's law within a range of fields, giving a linear graph, so R-3.\n(S) In a very strong field, every material, even germanium, departs from Ohm's law, so S-4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":40,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In a Wheatstone bridge, a cell is connected between corners A and C. A galvanometer is connected between corners B and D. Which of the following correctly names these two arms?","image":"","options":["AC is the galvanometer arm and BD is the battery arm","AC is the battery arm and BD is the galvanometer arm","Both AC and BD are called ratio arms","AC is the bridge arm and BD is the null arm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: in a Wheatstone bridge the source sits across one pair of diagonally opposite corners. That diagonal is called the battery arm. \nThe galvanometer, which detects current, joins the other two corners. That diagonal is called the galvanometer arm. \nSo AC is the battery arm and BD is the galvanometer arm. \nOption (1) reverses the two names, so it is wrong. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":41,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Twelve identical resistors, each of resistance 3 $\\Omega$, form the edges of a cube. A 15 V battery of negligible internal resistance is connected across two diagonally opposite corners A and C$'$ of the cube. What is the current in any edge that touches neither A nor C$'$?","image":"","options":["1 A","2 A","0.5 A","6 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: R = 3 $\\Omega$ per edge, $\\varepsilon$ = 15 V.\nBy symmetry, the three edges from A each carry I. At each next corner, I splits equally, so each of the six middle edges carries I/2.\nLoop rule along A → middle edge → C$'$: $\\varepsilon = IR + \\frac{I}{2}R + IR = \\frac{5}{2}IR$.\nSubstituting: 15 = 2.5 × I × 3, which gives I = 2 A. (The total current is 3I = 6 A, and $R_{eq} = \\frac{5}{6} \\times 3$ = 2.5 $\\Omega$.)\nSo the current in a middle edge is I/2 = 1 A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":42,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A cell of emf 2 V and internal resistance 1 $\\Omega$ is connected in parallel with another cell of emf 1 V and internal resistance 2 $\\Omega$. The positive terminals are joined together and the negative terminals are joined together. This combination is connected across an external resistor of 1 $\\Omega$. What is the current through the external resistor?","image":"","options":["1.2 A","0.6 A","1.8 A","1.0 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $\\varepsilon_1$ = 2 V, $r_1$ = 1 $\\Omega$, $\\varepsilon_2$ = 1 V, $r_2$ = 2 $\\Omega$, R = 1 $\\Omega$.\nFormula: $\\varepsilon_{eq} = \\frac{\\varepsilon_1 r_2 + \\varepsilon_2 r_1}{r_1 + r_2}$ and $r_{eq} = \\frac{r_1 r_2}{r_1 + r_2}$.\nSubstituting: $\\varepsilon_{eq} = \\frac{2 \\times 2 + 1 \\times 1}{3} = \\frac{5}{3}$ V and $r_{eq} = \\frac{2}{3}$ $\\Omega$.\nSo I = $\\frac{5/3}{1 + 2/3}$ = 1.0 A. Wrongly adding the emfs (3 V) would give 1.8 A.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":43,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Electric current is a scalar quantity even though it is represented by an arrow in circuit diagrams.\nReason (R): The current through an area is given by $I = \\vec{j} \\cdot \\Delta \\vec{S}$, the scalar product of the current density and the area vector.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The arrow only shows the sense of flow; currents do not obey vector addition.\nR is true. NCERT writes $I = \\vec{j} \\cdot \\Delta \\vec{S}$.\nThe dot product of two vectors is a scalar, so R directly explains why current is a scalar.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":44,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A cubical network has 12 identical resistors of 2 Ω each on its edges. A 12 V battery of negligible internal resistance is connected across two diagonally opposite corners. What is the current in any one of the six 'middle' edges, i.e., the edges that touch neither battery corner?","image":"","options":["0.6 A","2.4 A","3.6 A","1.2 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: R = 2 Ω, ε = 12 V.\nReq = (5/6)R = 5/3 Ω, so the total current = 12 ÷ (5/3) = 7.2 A.\nBy symmetry, the three edges at the battery corner share this current equally: I = 7.2/3 = 2.4 A each.\nAt each next corner, the current 2.4 A splits equally into two middle edges, giving 1.2 A in each.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":45,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A factory needs 100 kW of power. It is supplied through transmission cables of total resistance 0.4 $\\Omega$ at a voltage of 10 kV. How much power is wasted in the cables?","image":"","options":["4 W","40 W","400 W","4000 W"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $P = 10^5$ W, $R_c = 0.4\\ \\Omega$, $V = 10^4$ V.\nFormula: $P_c = \\frac{P^2R_c}{V^2}$.\nSubstituting: $P_c = \\frac{(10^5)^2 \\times 0.4}{(10^4)^2} = \\frac{0.4 \\times 10^{10}}{10^{8}}$.\nSo $P_c$ = 40 W.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":46,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (quantity, for electrons of charge magnitude e, mass m, number density n and relaxation time $\\tau$ in a field E) with List-II (expression):\nList-I\n(A) Magnitude of drift velocity\n(B) Mobility\n(C) Conductivity\n(D) Magnitude of current density\nList-II\n(I) $\\frac{ne^2\\tau}{m}$\n(II) $\\frac{eE\\tau}{m}$\n(III) $\\frac{e\\tau}{m}$\n(IV) $nev_d$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The drift velocity comes from acceleration $\\frac{eE}{m}$ acting for an average time $\\tau$, so $|v_d| = \\frac{eE\\tau}{m}$. This is II.\n(B) Mobility is $\\mu = \\frac{|v_d|}{E} = \\frac{e\\tau}{m}$. This is III.\n(C) Comparing $j = \\frac{ne^2\\tau}{m}E$ with $j = \\sigma E$ gives $\\sigma = \\frac{ne^2\\tau}{m}$. This is I.\n(D) Current density is $j = \\frac{I}{A} = nev_d$. This is IV.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":47,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The relation $V = IR$ can be used to find the resistance of a diode at any particular operating point, even though a diode is non-ohmic.\nStatement II: A device obeys Ohm's law only if its I versus V plot is linear, i.e., R is independent of V.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. $V = IR$ is a definition of resistance. It can be applied to any conducting device, ohmic or not, to get V/I at a given point.\nStatement II is correct. Ohm's law makes a stronger claim: the I-V plot is a straight line, so R does not change with V.\nA diode has a V/I value at each point, but that value changes with V. So the diode fails Ohm's law.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":48,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (physical quantity) with List-II (SI unit).\nList-I:\n(A) Current density\n(B) Mobility\n(C) Resistivity\n(D) Relaxation time\nList-II:\n(p) $\\Omega$ m\n(q) s\n(r) A $m^{-2}$\n(s) $m^2 V^{-1} s^{-1}$","image":"","options":["A-s, B-r, C-p, D-q","A-r, B-s, C-p, D-q","A-r, B-p, C-s, D-q","A-q, B-s, C-p, D-r"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Current density is current per unit area, so its unit is A $m^{-2}$. Match: r.\n(B) Mobility is $|v_d|/E$, which gives (m/s)/(V/m) = $m^2V^{-1}s^{-1}$. Match: s.\n(C) Resistivity comes from $\\rho = RA/l$, so its unit is $\\Omega$ m. Match: p.\n(D) Relaxation time is an average time between collisions, measured in s. Match: q.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":49,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Electric current is a scalar quantity even though it is shown with an arrow in circuit diagrams.\nStatement II: The equation $V = IR$ is itself the statement of Ohm's law, so every device to which it can be applied obeys Ohm's law.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Currents do not add like vectors, and $I = \\vec{j} \\cdot \\Delta\\vec{S}$ is a scalar product, which gives a scalar.\nStatement II is incorrect. $V = IR$ only defines resistance and can be applied even to a diode. Ohm's law states that R is independent of V, which means the I–V graph is linear.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":50,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In a metallic conductor carrying a steady current, charge carriers lose electric potential energy as they move from the higher-potential end to the lower-potential end. Yet their average drift speed stays constant. What happens to this lost potential energy?","image":"","options":["It is converted into the kinetic energy of the electrons, so their drift speed keeps increasing along the wire","It is passed on to the atoms of the conductor during collisions, making them vibrate more vigorously, so the conductor heats up","It is stored as extra charge at the far end of the conductor","It is returned to the cell, so no net energy is used in the circuit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In time $\\Delta t$, a charge $\\Delta Q = I\\Delta t$ falls through a potential difference V. Its potential energy changes by $\\Delta U = -IV\\Delta t$.\nIf the charges moved freely, this energy would become kinetic energy. However, collisions with ions keep the drift velocity steady on average.\nDuring these collisions, the energy is shared with the atoms. The atoms vibrate more vigorously, so the conductor heats up (Joule heating). The energy dissipated is $IV\\Delta t$.\nOption (1) fails because the drift speed does not keep increasing.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":51,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A rectangular slab of resistance R is cut lengthwise into two identical half-slabs. Each half has the same length but half the cross-sectional area. The same potential difference V is applied across the full slab and across each half.\nStatement I: Each half-slab has resistance 2R.\nStatement II: If I is the current through the full slab, the current through each half-slab is $\\frac{I}{2}$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct. The full current I divides equally between the two identical halves, so each half carries $\\frac{I}{2}$.\nStatement I is correct. Each half has voltage V across it and carries $\\frac{I}{2}$, so its resistance is $\\frac{V}{I/2} = 2R$.\nThis is the NCERT slab argument showing that halving the cross-sectional area doubles the resistance, i.e. $R \\propto \\frac{1}{A}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":52,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A copper wire is 2.0 m long and has a uniform cross-sectional area of $1.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$. The resistivity of copper is $1.7 \\times 10^{-8}$ $\\Omega$ m. What is the resistance of the wire?","image":"","options":["0.034 $\\Omega$","0.0085 $\\Omega$","0.068 $\\Omega$","0.34 $\\Omega$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $l$ = 2.0 m, $A = 1.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$, $\\rho = 1.7 \\times 10^{-8}$ $\\Omega$ m.\nFormula: $R = \\frac{\\rho l}{A}$.\nSubstituting: $R = \\frac{1.7 \\times 10^{-8} \\times 2.0}{1.0 \\times 10^{-6}} = 3.4 \\times 10^{-2}$ $\\Omega$.\nSo R = 0.034 $\\Omega$. Option (2) comes from dividing by the length instead of multiplying by it. Option (4) is a power-of-ten slip.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":53,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If a current labelled $I_2$ with an arrow along a resistor comes out negative after solving Kirchhoff's equations, the actual current flows opposite to the arrow.\nStatement II: The arrow directions must be guessed correctly before writing Kirchhoff's equations, otherwise the equations give wrong magnitudes of current.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In NCERT's labelling procedure, a negative value simply means the current flows opposite to the assumed arrow.\nStatement II is incorrect. Arrow directions can be chosen arbitrarily. A wrong guess only flips the sign of the result, and the magnitude still comes out correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":54,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In a metal at $20^\\circ$C, the relaxation time of conduction electrons is $2.5\\times10^{-14}$ s. The temperature coefficient of resistivity of the metal is $4.0\\times10^{-3}\\ ^\\circ\\text{C}^{-1}$. If the metal is heated to $70^\\circ$C, and the number density of free electrons is assumed to stay constant, what is the new relaxation time (in units of $10^{-14}$ s)?","image":"","options":["3.0","2.08","2.5","1.25"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\tau_1 = 2.5\\times10^{-14}$ s, $\\alpha = 4.0\\times10^{-3}\\ ^\\circ$C$^{-1}$, $\\Delta T = 50^\\circ$C.\nResistivity ratio: $\\frac{\\rho_2}{\\rho_1} = 1 + \\alpha\\Delta T = 1.2$.\nFormula: $\\rho = \\frac{m}{ne^2\\tau}$; with $n$ constant, $\\tau \\propto \\frac{1}{\\rho}$.\nSubstituting: $\\tau_2 = \\frac{2.5}{1.2}\\times10^{-14} \\approx 2.08\\times10^{-14}$ s.\nOption (1) wrongly treats $\\tau$ as proportional to $\\rho$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":55,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Three branches are connected in parallel between two junctions X and Y.\nBranch 1: a 10 V cell (negligible internal resistance) in series with 2 Ω.\nBranch 2: a 6 V cell (negligible internal resistance) in series with 3 Ω.\nBranch 3: a 6 Ω resistor.\nBoth cells have their positive terminals towards X. Using Kirchhoff's rules, find the current in the 6 Ω resistor.","image":"","options":["2.0 A","1.17 A","0.33 A","1.6 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Let V = V(X) − V(Y).\nUsing the loop rule, the branch currents towards X are (10 − V)/2 and (6 − V)/3. The current from X to Y through branch 3 is V/6.\nJunction rule at X: (10 − V)/2 + (6 − V)/3 = V/6, which gives 42 = 6V, so V = 7 V.\nCurrent in 6 Ω = 7/6 ≈ 1.17 A.\nThe current in branch 2 is (6 − 7)/3 = −1/3 A. The negative sign means the 6 V cell is actually being charged.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":56,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A wire of cross-sectional area $2.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$ carries a steady current of 4.0 A distributed uniformly over its cross-section. The resistivity of its material is $1.6 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\Omega$ m. What is the magnitude of the electric field inside the wire?","image":"","options":["0.016 V/m","0.032 V/m","0.064 V/m","0.0032 V/m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $I = 4.0$ A, $A = 2.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$, $\\rho = 1.6 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\Omega$ m.\nFormula: $j = I/A$ and $E = \\rho j$.\nSubstituting: $j = 4.0/(2.0 \\times 10^{-6}) = 2.0 \\times 10^{6}$ A/m$^2$. Then $E = 1.6 \\times 10^{-8} \\times 2.0 \\times 10^{6} = 3.2 \\times 10^{-2}$ V/m.\nSo E = 0.032 V/m.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":57,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about free electrons in a metal:\n(i) With no electric field, the paths of electrons between successive collisions are straight lines.\n(ii) With an electric field present, the paths between collisions are, in general, curved.\n(iii) When a current flows, all free electrons move in the same direction, from lower to higher potential.\n(iv) An electron keeps the drift velocity it had gained even after colliding with an ion.\n(v) With no electric field, the average velocity of all the free electrons is zero.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["3","2","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: with no force acting, electrons travel in straight lines between collisions.\n(ii) Correct: the field accelerates the electrons, so their paths generally bend.\n(iii) Incorrect: the small drift is superposed on large random velocities, so electrons do not all move the same way.\n(iv) Incorrect: an electron loses its drift speed in a collision and then accelerates again.\n(v) Correct: velocity directions after collisions are random, so $\\frac{1}{N}\\sum v_i = 0$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":58,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"GaAs is quoted in NCERT as a material that does not obey Ohm's law. Which feature of its current-voltage behaviour is responsible?","image":"","options":["V is not proportional to I, but each I has exactly one V","The current for a voltage V differs in magnitude from the current for $-V$","There is more than one value of V for the same current I","Its resistance is zero at all voltages"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists three kinds of deviation from Ohm's law:\n(a) a nonlinear V-I relation;\n(b) the relation depends on the sign of V, as in a diode;\n(c) the V-I relation is not unique.\nGaAs is the example given for (c): its curve has more than one value of V for the same I.\nThe sign dependence in option (2) describes a diode, not GaAs.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":59,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A copper wire 4.0 m long with cross-sectional area $2.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$ carries a steady current of 3.0 A. The potential difference across its ends is 0.12 V. The free-electron density is $8.5 \\times 10^{28}$ m$^{-3}$ and $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C. What is the electron mobility in cm$^2$/Vs?","image":"","options":["3.68 cm$^2$/Vs","36.8 cm$^2$/Vs","368 cm$^2$/Vs","0.368 cm$^2$/Vs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: l = 4.0 m, A = $2.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$, I = 3.0 A, V = 0.12 V, n = $8.5 \\times 10^{28}$ m$^{-3}$.\nFormula: $v_d = \\frac{I}{neA}$, $E = \\frac{V}{l}$, $\\mu = \\frac{v_d}{E}$.\nSubstituting: $v_d = \\frac{3.0}{8.5 \\times 10^{28} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 2.0 \\times 10^{-6}} \\approx 1.10 \\times 10^{-4}$ m/s.\nThe field is E = 0.12/4.0 = 0.030 V/m.\nSo $\\mu \\approx 3.68 \\times 10^{-3}$ m$^2$/Vs = 36.8 cm$^2$/Vs.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":60,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about electric current is correct?","image":"","options":["Current is a vector because it always has a direction along the wire.","Currents meeting at a junction add according to the parallelogram law.","Current is a scalar, although it is shown with an arrow, because it does not obey the law of vector addition.","Current is a vector because it is obtained from the current density vector $\\vec{j}$."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Current has a sense of flow, so we mark it with an arrow. However, currents do not combine by vector addition.\nAt a junction, currents simply add algebraically, whatever the angle between the wires.\nAlso, $I = \\vec{j} \\cdot \\Delta \\vec{S}$ is a scalar product, so its result is a scalar.\nHaving a direction of flow alone does not make a quantity a vector.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":61,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about applying Kirchhoff's rules.\n(i) If a branch current labelled with an arrow comes out negative after solving, the actual current flows opposite to the arrow.\n(ii) The junction rule applies equally to any single point on a wire, not only to a meeting point of several wires.\n(iii) Bending or reorienting a wire at a junction can make the junction rule invalid.\n(iv) Once enough independent loop equations are solved, applying the loop rule to further loops gives no new independent equation.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. A negative result simply means the assumed direction was wrong.\n(ii) Correct. Charge cannot accumulate at any point of a line in steady state.\n(iii) Incorrect. Current is a scalar, so the geometry of the wires does not affect the junction rule.\n(iv) Correct. As in the multi-loop example, the currents found already satisfy every other loop.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":62,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) $q = q_+ - q_-$\n(B) $I = \\frac{q}{t}$\n(C) $I = \\lim_{\\Delta t \\to 0} \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t}$\n(D) $I = \\vec{j} \\cdot \\Delta \\vec{S}$\nList-II:\n(i) Current at a given instant when the flow is not steady\n(ii) Net charge crossing an area in the forward direction\n(iii) Current through an area expressed as a scalar product of two vectors\n(iv) Current when the net charge crossing is proportional to time","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii","A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)-(ii): $q_+ - q_-$ gives the net charge moving forward across the area.\n(B)-(iv): $I = \\frac{q}{t}$ applies to steady current, where q is proportional to t.\n(C)-(i): The limit definition gives the current at an instant, so it also works for non-steady currents.\n(D)-(iii): $\\vec{j} \\cdot \\Delta \\vec{S}$ is a dot product of two vectors.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":63,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"For a metal, the simple drift model gives the conductivity as $\\sigma = \\frac{ne^2\\tau}{m}$. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A metal with more free electrons per unit volume has a higher conductivity, other factors being equal.\n(ii) If the relaxation time doubles, the conductivity doubles.\n(iii) A longer wire of the same metal has a lower conductivity.\n(iv) In a metal, raising the temperature makes collisions more frequent, which lowers $\\tau$ and hence $\\sigma$.\n(v) In this model, $\\sigma$ comes out independent of $E$, which reproduces Ohm's law $\\vec{j} = \\sigma\\vec{E}$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $\\sigma \\propto n$.\n(ii) Correct: $\\sigma \\propto \\tau$.\n(iii) Incorrect: $\\sigma$ is a property of the material. The resistance depends on length, but the conductivity does not.\n(iv) Correct: faster electrons collide more often, so $\\tau$ falls; n stays nearly constant in a metal.\n(v) Correct: with n and $\\tau$ assumed independent of E, we get $\\vec{j} \\propto \\vec{E}$, which is Ohm's law.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":64,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A power station must deliver 100 kW to a distant town through transmission cables of total resistance $5\\ \\Omega$. When the power is transmitted at 10 kV, some power is wasted in the cables. If the transmission voltage is raised to 50 kV, with the same power delivered and the same cables, what is the power wasted in the cables?","image":"","options":["100 W","2500 W","20 W","4 W"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $P = 10^5$ W, $R_c = 5\\ \\Omega$, $V = 5\\times10^4$ V.\nFormula: The line current is $I = P/V$, so the cable loss is $P_c = I^2R_c = \\frac{P^2R_c}{V^2}$.\nSubstituting: $P_c = \\frac{(10^5)^2\\times5}{(5\\times10^4)^2} = \\frac{5\\times10^{10}}{2.5\\times10^9} = 20$ W.\nCheck: at 10 kV the loss would be 500 W. Raising V five times cuts the loss by $5^2 = 25$ times. The value 100 W wrongly assumes the loss falls as $1/V$.\nSo the power wasted = 20 W.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":65,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A wire of uniform cross-sectional area $4.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$ carries a steady current of 2.0 A, distributed uniformly over the cross-section. What is the magnitude of the current density in the wire?","image":"","options":["$5.0 \\times 10^5$ A/m$^2$","$2.0 \\times 10^6$ A/m$^2$","$5.0 \\times 10^4$ A/m$^2$","$8.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ A/m$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: I = 2.0 A, $A = 4.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$.\nFormula: current density $j = \\frac{I}{A}$, measured normal to the current.\nSubstituting: $j = \\frac{2.0}{4.0 \\times 10^{-6}} = 0.5 \\times 10^6$ A/m$^2$.\nSo $j = 5.0 \\times 10^5$ A/m$^2$. Option (4) comes from multiplying I by A instead of dividing.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":66,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a cell sends current through an external resistor, the potential difference between its terminals is less than its emf.\nReason (R): Inside the cell the current flows through the electrolyte from the negative to the positive electrode, and the electrolyte's internal resistance r causes a potential drop Ir.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: while the cell discharges, $V = \\varepsilon - Ir < \\varepsilon$.\nR is true: during discharge, current flows from N to P through the electrolyte.\nThe electrolyte has a finite internal resistance r, so there is a drop Ir between B and A.\nThis drop is exactly the term subtracted from $\\varepsilon$, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":67,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Wire-bound standard resistors are commonly made of manganin or constantan. The main reason is that these alloys:","image":"","options":["show a very weak dependence of resistivity on temperature","have resistivity as low as that of pure copper","have a negative temperature coefficient, like semiconductors","obey Ohm's law only at very low temperatures"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Like nichrome, manganin and constantan show only a very weak variation of resistivity with temperature.\nBecause of this, the resistance of a standard resistor stays almost constant even if its temperature changes.\nThese alloys do not have resistivity as low as copper, so option (2) is wrong.\nThey are not semiconductors and do not have a negative coefficient, so option (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":68,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In an electrolytic solution, the following charges cross a fixed cross-section during 4.0 s:\n5.0 C of positive charge moves forward and 1.0 C of positive charge moves backward.\n2.0 C of negative charge (magnitude) moves forward and 4.0 C of negative charge (magnitude) moves backward.\nWhat is the magnitude of the current in the forward direction?","image":"","options":["0.5 A","1.0 A","1.5 A","3.0 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: net positive charge moving forward, $q_+ = 5.0 - 1.0 = 4.0$ C. Net negative charge moving forward, $q_- = 2.0 - 4.0 = -2.0$ C, which means 2.0 C of negative charge moves backward.\nFormula: $q = q_+ - q_-$ and $I = \\frac{q}{t}$\nSubstituting: $q = 4.0 - (-2.0) = 6.0$ C. Negative charge moving backward adds to the forward current.\nSo $I = \\frac{6.0}{4.0} = 1.5$ A\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":69,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The electromotive force (emf) of a cell is a potential difference and not a force.\nStatement II: The emf of a cell equals the potential difference between its terminals while the cell is delivering current to an external resistor.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Emf is measured in volts, so it is a potential difference. The name 'force' survives only for historical reasons.\nStatement II is incorrect. Emf is the terminal potential difference in open circuit, when no current flows.\nWhen a current I flows, the terminal voltage is $V = \\varepsilon - Ir$, which is less than $\\varepsilon$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":70,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The resistivity of a metal increases with temperature mainly because the relaxation time $\\tau$ decreases, while the free-electron density $n$ stays practically constant.\nStatement II: Nichrome is used in standard resistors because its resistivity changes strongly with temperature.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. From $\\rho = \\frac{m}{ne^2\\tau}$, $\\tau$ falls as collisions become more frequent at higher temperature. In a metal, $n$ hardly changes, so $\\rho$ rises.\nStatement II is incorrect. Nichrome, manganin and constantan show a very weak temperature dependence of resistivity. That is exactly why they are used in standard resistors.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":71,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A power station delivers a fixed power P to a distant town through cables of fixed resistance. When transmission is done at 11 kV, the power wasted in the cables is 40 kW. If the same power is transmitted at 220 kV through the same cables, what will be the power wasted in the cables?","image":"","options":["2000 W","100 W","800 W","10 W"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: P and $R_c$ are fixed; $P_c$ = 40 kW at 11 kV.\nFormula: $P_c = \\frac{P^2 R_c}{V^2}$, so $P_c \\propto \\frac{1}{V^2}$.\nSubstituting: the voltage rises by 220/11 = 20 times, so the loss falls by $20^2$ = 400 times. New loss = 40000/400.\nSo $P_c$ = 100 W. The option 2000 W wrongly assumes $P_c \\propto 1/V$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":72,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Two identical cells, each of emf $\\varepsilon$ and internal resistance r, are connected in parallel between points A and B. The positive terminal of the first cell and the negative terminal of the second cell are joined at A, and the remaining terminals are joined at B. Which statement correctly describes the equivalent single cell between A and B?","image":"","options":["Emf $\\varepsilon$ and internal resistance 2r","Emf zero and internal resistance $\\frac{r}{2}$","Emf zero and internal resistance 2r","Emf $\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}$ and internal resistance $\\frac{r}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"For a parallel combination: $\\frac{1}{r_{eq}} = \\frac{1}{r_1}+\\frac{1}{r_2}$ and $\\frac{\\varepsilon_{eq}}{r_{eq}} = \\frac{\\varepsilon_1}{r_1}+\\frac{\\varepsilon_2}{r_2}$. A reversed cell enters with $\\varepsilon_2 \\to -\\varepsilon_2$.\nInternal resistance: $r_{eq} = \\frac{r\\cdot r}{2r} = \\frac{r}{2}$. Reversing a cell does not change the resistances.\nEmf: $\\frac{\\varepsilon_{eq}}{r/2} = \\frac{\\varepsilon}{r} - \\frac{\\varepsilon}{r} = 0$, so $\\varepsilon_{eq} = 0$.\nA current $\\frac{2\\varepsilon}{2r} = \\frac{\\varepsilon}{r}$ circulates between the cells and wastes them.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":73,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A cell of emf 2.0 V and internal resistance 0.5 $\\Omega$ is connected in parallel with a cell of emf 1.5 V and internal resistance 1 $\\Omega$, with like terminals joined. The combination is connected across an external resistor R. For what value of R will no current flow through the 1.5 V cell?","image":"","options":["3.0 $\\Omega$","1.5 $\\Omega$","0.75 $\\Omega$","1.0 $\\Omega$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) The current through the 1.5 V cell is zero when its terminal voltage equals its emf: V = $\\varepsilon_2 - I_2 r_2$ = 1.5 V with $I_2$ = 0.\n(ii) The parallel cells share the same terminal voltage, so for the 2 V cell: 1.5 = 2.0 − $I_1$(0.5). This gives $I_1$ = 1 A.\n(iii) The entire 1 A passes through R, so R = V/I = 1.5/1 = 1.5 $\\Omega$.\n(iv) Check: $\\varepsilon_{eq}$ = (2×1 + 1.5×0.5)/1.5 = 11/6 V and $r_{eq}$ = 1/3 $\\Omega$, so I = (11/6)/(1.5 + 1/3) = 1 A, which is consistent.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":74,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In a copper wire carrying a current of a few amperes, three speeds are compared: (a) the drift speed of conduction electrons, (b) the typical thermal speed of copper atoms at 300 K, and (c) the speed at which the electric field travels along the wire. Which option gives their correct increasing order?","image":"","options":["(a) < (b) < (c)","(b) < (a) < (c)","(a) < (c) < (b)","(c) < (b) < (a)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Drift speed in copper is about 1 mm/s, of the order of $10^{-3}$ m/s.\nThe thermal speed of copper atoms at 300 K is about $2 \\times 10^{2}$ m/s.\nThe electric field travels along the wire at the speed of an electromagnetic wave, $3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.\nSo the order is drift < thermal < field propagation.\nOption (2) fails because drift speed is about $10^{-5}$ times the thermal speed, so it is not larger.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":75,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In an electrolytic cell, the positive electrode is at a potential of +1.1 V relative to the electrolyte next to it. The negative electrode is at a potential of –0.4 V relative to the electrolyte next to it. What is the emf of the cell?","image":"","options":["0.7 V","1.5 V","1.1 V","0.44 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $V_+ = 1.1$ V and $V_- = 0.4$ V.\nWith no current, the electrolyte has the same potential throughout.\nSo the potential difference between P and N is $V_+ - (-V_-) = V_+ + V_-$.\nFormula: $\\varepsilon = V_+ + V_-$\nSubstituting: $\\varepsilon = 1.1 + 0.4 = 1.5$ V.\nOption (1) wrongly subtracts the two magnitudes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":76,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about Kirchhoff's rules are correct?\n(a) The junction rule is a consequence of conservation of charge for steady currents.\n(b) The loop rule holds because electric potential has a definite value at each point, so the net change around a closed path is zero.\n(c) The junction rule can be applied even at a single point on a wire, not only where several wires meet.\n(d) Bending or reorienting a wire at a junction can make the junction rule invalid.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: for steady currents no charge accumulates at a junction, so current in equals current out.\n(b) Correct: potential depends only on location, so returning to the starting point gives zero net change.\n(c) Correct: NCERT states the rule applies equally to a point in a line.\n(d) Incorrect: bending or reorienting the wire does not affect the junction rule, since current is a scalar.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":77,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Two identical cells, each of emf $\\varepsilon$ and internal resistance r, are combined in different ways. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) In series (positive of one to negative of other): $\\varepsilon_{eq} = 2\\varepsilon$, $r_{eq} = 2r$\n(b) In parallel (like terminals joined): $\\varepsilon_{eq} = \\varepsilon$, $r_{eq} = r/2$\n(c) In series with one cell reversed: $\\varepsilon_{eq} = 0$ and $r_{eq} = 0$\n(d) The maximum current a single cell can drive is $\\varepsilon/r$\n(e) In parallel with one cell reversed: $\\varepsilon_{eq} = 0$, $r_{eq} = r/2$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: in series both emfs and internal resistances add.\n(b) Correct: $\\frac{1}{r_{eq}} = \\frac{2}{r}$ gives $r/2$, and $\\frac{\\varepsilon_{eq}}{r_{eq}} = \\frac{2\\varepsilon}{r}$ gives $\\varepsilon_{eq} = \\varepsilon$.\n(c) Incorrect: the emfs cancel, but internal resistances still add, so $r_{eq} = 2r$.\n(d) Correct: with R = 0, $I_{max} = \\varepsilon/r$.\n(e) Correct: replacing $\\varepsilon$ by $-\\varepsilon$ gives $\\frac{\\varepsilon}{r} - \\frac{\\varepsilon}{r} = 0$, while $r_{eq}$ stays $r/2$.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":78,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In a Wheatstone bridge, the four arms are AB = 4 $\\Omega$, BC = 6 $\\Omega$, AD = 8 $\\Omega$ and DC = X. A cell is connected between A and C, and a galvanometer is connected between B and D. The galvanometer shows null deflection. What is the value of X?","image":"","options":["12 $\\Omega$","3 $\\Omega$","5.3 $\\Omega$","2 $\\Omega$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: AB = 4 $\\Omega$, BC = 6 $\\Omega$, AD = 8 $\\Omega$, DC = X, and the galvanometer reads zero.\nFormula: at balance, $\\frac{R_{AB}}{R_{BC}} = \\frac{R_{AD}}{R_{DC}}$, because B and D are then at the same potential.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{4}{6} = \\frac{8}{X}$, so $X = \\frac{8 \\times 6}{4}$.\nSo X = 12 $\\Omega$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":79,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A metal wire is stretched uniformly to twice its original length, keeping the temperature constant. Its resistance becomes four times the original value.\nReason (R): On stretching, the resistivity of the metal stays the same, and since the volume is unchanged, the cross-sectional area becomes half.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. Resistivity is a property of the material, so stretching does not change it. Since volume $Al$ is constant, doubling l halves A.\nA is true. $R' = \\frac{\\rho (2l)}{A/2} = 4\\frac{\\rho l}{A} = 4R$.\nThe factor 4 arises exactly because l doubles and A halves while ρ stays fixed. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":80,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A metal wire of cross-sectional area $2.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$ carries a current of 2.0 A. The number density of free electrons is $8.0 \\times 10^{28}$ m$^{-3}$ and $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C. What is the drift speed of the electrons?","image":"","options":["0.078 mm/s","0.78 mm/s","0.0078 mm/s","7.8 mm/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: I = 2.0 A, n = $8.0 \\times 10^{28}$ m$^{-3}$, A = $2.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$.\nFormula: $v_d = \\frac{I}{neA}$\nSubstituting: $v_d = \\frac{2.0}{8.0 \\times 10^{28} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 2.0 \\times 10^{-6}} = \\frac{2.0}{2.56 \\times 10^{4}}$\nThis gives $v_d \\approx 7.8 \\times 10^{-5}$ m/s.\nSo $v_d \\approx$ 0.078 mm/s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":81,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Battery arm\n(b) Galvanometer arm\n(c) Null deflection\n(d) Meter bridge\nList-II\n(i) Practical device using the bridge principle to measure an unknown resistance\n(ii) Arm joining the two corners across which the cell is connected\n(iii) Arm containing the current detector, joining the remaining two corners\n(iv) Condition $I_g = 0$","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i","a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i","a-ii, b-iii, c-i, d-iv","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) The battery arm is the diagonal across which the source is connected, so a-ii. \n(b) The galvanometer arm holds the current detector between the other two corners, so b-iii. \n(c) Null deflection means no current through the galvanometer, so c-iv. \n(d) The meter bridge is the practical device based on this principle, so d-i. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":82,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Over a limited range of temperature, the graph of resistivity of copper against temperature is nearly a straight line, but at temperatures much lower than $0^\\circ$C it deviates considerably from a straight line.\nStatement II: Adding a small amount of a suitable impurity to a semiconductor increases its resistivity.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The relation $\\rho_T = \\rho_0[1+\\alpha(T-T_0)]$ gives a straight line only over a limited range. Fig. 3.8 shows that the graph bends away from linear well below $0^\\circ$C.\nStatement II is incorrect. Suitable impurities decrease the resistivity of a semiconductor. This feature is exploited in electronic devices.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":83,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A cell of unknown emf $\\varepsilon$ and internal resistance r is connected to an external resistor of 4 Ω, and the current is 1.0 A. When the resistor is replaced by one of 9 Ω, the current becomes 0.5 A. What is the maximum current that can be drawn from this cell?","image":"","options":["5 A","1 A","2.5 A","10 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $I_1 = 1.0$ A with $R_1 = 4\\ \\Omega$, and $I_2 = 0.5$ A with $R_2 = 9\\ \\Omega$.\nFormula: $\\varepsilon = I(R + r)$, which gives $1.0(4 + r) = 0.5(9 + r)$.\nSolving: $4 + r = 4.5 + 0.5r$, so $r = 1\\ \\Omega$ and $\\varepsilon = 5$ V.\nThe maximum current is drawn when R = 0: $I_{max} = \\frac{\\varepsilon}{r} = \\frac{5}{1} = 5$ A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":84,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform wire of resistance 16 Ω is drawn out uniformly until its diameter becomes half the original value. The temperature and volume stay unchanged. The new wire is then cut into 4 equal pieces, and these pieces are connected in parallel. What is the equivalent resistance of the combination?","image":"","options":["64 Ω","4 Ω","16 Ω","256 Ω"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $R = 16\\ \\Omega$ and the diameter is halved, so $A' = A/4$.\nVolume is constant, so $l' = 4l$.\nFormula: $R' = \\frac{\\rho l'}{A'} = \\frac{\\rho (4l)}{A/4} = 16R = 256\\ \\Omega$.\nEach of the 4 equal pieces has resistance $256/4 = 64\\ \\Omega$.\nFour equal resistances in parallel give $64/4 = 16\\ \\Omega$.\nSo the equivalent resistance is 16 Ω.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":85,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Two copper wires P and Q are joined end to end and carry a steady current. Wire P has diameter 2d and wire Q has diameter d. Consider the following statements:\n(i) The current in P and Q is the same.\n(ii) The drift speed of electrons in Q is 4 times that in P.\n(iii) The electric field inside Q is 4 times that inside P.\n(iv) The mobility of electrons in Q is 4 times that in P.\nWhich of the statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i) and (ii) only","(i), (ii) and (iii) only","(ii), (iii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the wires are in series, so by the junction rule the current is the same in both.\n(ii) Correct: $v_d = \\frac{I}{neA}$ with the same n and I. Q has 1/4 the area of P, so its drift speed is 4 times larger.\n(iii) Correct: $E = \\rho j$, and j in Q is 4 times that in P.\n(iv) Incorrect: $\\mu = \\frac{e\\tau}{m}$ depends only on the material, so it is the same in both.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":86,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In an electrolytic cell, the positive electrode P is at a potential of +0.9 V relative to the electrolyte just next to it. The negative electrode N is at a potential of −0.6 V relative to the electrolyte just next to it. No current is drawn from the cell. What is the emf of the cell?","image":"","options":["0.3 V","1.5 V","0.9 V","0.54 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $V_+ = 0.9$ V and $V_- = 0.6$ V. N is at $-V_-$ relative to the electrolyte next to it.\nFormula: with no current, the electrolyte has the same potential throughout. So $\\varepsilon = V_+ - (-V_-) = V_+ + V_-$.\nSubstituting: $\\varepsilon = 0.9 + 0.6 = 1.5$ V.\nThe tempting value 0.3 V comes from subtracting the magnitudes. That ignores the fact that N is below the electrolyte while P is above it.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":87,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In a Wheatstone bridge, $R_1 = 10\\,\\Omega$ and $R_2 = 20\\,\\Omega$. The unknown arm $R_4$ is a metal coil kept at 20 °C, and the bridge balances when $R_3 = 15\\,\\Omega$. The coil is then heated to 70 °C, and balance is restored by changing $R_3$ to $18\\,\\Omega$. Using the balance condition $\\frac{R_2}{R_1} = \\frac{R_4}{R_3}$, find the temperature coefficient of resistance of the coil (taking 20 °C as reference).","image":"","options":["$3.3 \\times 10^{-3}$ °C$^{-1}$","$4.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ °C$^{-1}$","$2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ °C$^{-1}$","$8.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ °C$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $R_2/R_1 = 2$, $R_3 = 15\\,\\Omega$ at 20 °C and $18\\,\\Omega$ at 70 °C.\nFormula: $R_4 = R_3\\frac{R_2}{R_1}$ and $R_T = R_0[1 + \\alpha(T - T_0)]$.\nSubstituting: $R_4$ = 30 Ω at 20 °C and 36 Ω at 70 °C.\n$\\alpha = \\frac{36 - 30}{30 \\times 50} = 4.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ °C$^{-1}$.\nThe distractor $3.3 \\times 10^{-3}$ wrongly divides by the hot resistance (36 Ω) instead of the reference resistance.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":88,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In a Wheatstone bridge ABCD, the arms have resistances AB = 2 Ω, BC = 4 Ω, AD = 3 Ω and DC = 6 Ω. A galvanometer of resistance 5 Ω is connected between B and D. A 9 V battery of negligible internal resistance is connected across A and C. What current is drawn from the battery?","image":"","options":["1.0 A","1.5 A","3.75 A","2.5 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{AB}{BC} = \\frac{2}{4}$ and $\\frac{AD}{DC} = \\frac{3}{6}$. The ratios are equal, so the bridge is balanced and the galvanometer carries no current. \nFormula: ignore BD. Path ABC = 6 Ω is in parallel with path ADC = 9 Ω, so $R_{eq} = \\frac{6\\times 9}{6+9}$. \nSubstituting: $R_{eq} = 3.6\\ \\Omega$ and $I = \\frac{9}{3.6}$. \nSo I = 2.5 A. \nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":89,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (material) with List-II (resistivity behaviour).\nList-I:\n(a) Copper\n(b) Nichrome\n(c) Germanium\n(d) Rubber\nList-II:\n(p) Resistivity falls as temperature rises, and can be lowered by adding small amounts of suitable impurities\n(q) Resistivity is nearly independent of temperature; used in wire-bound standard resistors\n(r) Resistivity rises nearly linearly with temperature near room temperature, but the graph deviates from a straight line far below $0\\,^\\circ C$\n(s) Resistivity is about $10^{18}$ times that of metals or more","image":"","options":["(a)-(q), (b)-(r), (c)-(p), (d)-(s)","(a)-(r), (b)-(q), (c)-(s), (d)-(p)","(a)-(r), (b)-(p), (c)-(q), (d)-(s)","(a)-(r), (b)-(q), (c)-(p), (d)-(s)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(a) Copper → (r): $\\rho$ is nearly linear in $T$ around room temperature, but the curve bends at very low temperatures (Fig. 3.8).\n(b) Nichrome → (q): it shows a very weak temperature dependence.\n(c) Germanium → (p): as a semiconductor, its $\\rho$ falls with $T$ and doping lowers it.\n(d) Rubber → (s): it is an insulator with resistivity about $10^{18}$ times that of metals or more.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":90,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Consider n identical cells, each of emf $\\varepsilon$ and internal resistance r, connected in parallel with all positive terminals joined together and all negative terminals joined together.\nStatement I: The equivalent emf of the combination is $\\varepsilon$.\nStatement II: The equivalent internal resistance of the combination is nr.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Using $\\frac{1}{r_{eq}} = \\sum \\frac{1}{r_i}$: here $\\frac{1}{r_{eq}} = \\frac{n}{r}$, so $r_{eq} = \\frac{r}{n}$, not nr. Statement II is incorrect.\n(ii) Using $\\frac{\\varepsilon_{eq}}{r_{eq}} = \\sum \\frac{\\varepsilon_i}{r_i} = \\frac{n\\varepsilon}{r}$: this gives $\\varepsilon_{eq} = \\frac{n\\varepsilon}{r} \\cdot \\frac{r}{n} = \\varepsilon$. Statement I is correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":91,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A metal wire has a resistance of $50\\,\\Omega$ at $27\\,^\\circ C$. The temperature coefficient of resistance of the metal is $4.0 \\times 10^{-3}\\,^\\circ C^{-1}$. Assuming the linear relation holds, what is the resistance of the wire at $77\\,^\\circ C$?","image":"","options":["$40\\,\\Omega$","$55\\,\\Omega$","$60\\,\\Omega$","$70\\,\\Omega$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $R_1 = 50\\,\\Omega$, $\\alpha = 4.0\\times10^{-3}\\,^\\circ C^{-1}$, $\\Delta T = 77 - 27 = 50\\,^\\circ C$.\nFormula: $R_2 = R_1[1 + \\alpha\\,\\Delta T]$\nSubstituting: $R_2 = 50\\,[1 + 4.0\\times10^{-3}\\times 50] = 50 \\times 1.2$\nSo $R_2 = 60\\,\\Omega$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":92,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A metal wire is not connected to any source, so no electric field acts inside it. Its free electrons are moving randomly at high thermal speeds. Why is there no net electric current in the wire?","image":"","options":["After each collision with an ion, the electron's direction is random, so the average velocity of all electrons is zero","The free electrons are at rest when no electric field is applied","The free electrons stay bound to their parent nuclei when there is no field","Collisions with the fixed ions bring every electron's speed to zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"An electron leaves each collision with an ion at the same speed, but in a completely random direction.\nSo at any instant, as many electrons move in one direction as in the opposite direction.\nThis gives $\\frac{1}{N}\\sum v_i = 0$, so no net charge crosses any area.\nOption (2) fails because the electrons are not at rest; they have large thermal speeds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":93,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Two cells of emfs $\\varepsilon_1$, $\\varepsilon_2$ and internal resistances $r_1$, $r_2$ are connected in parallel. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) With like terminals joined, the equivalent internal resistance is smaller than both $r_1$ and $r_2$.\n(ii) With like terminals joined, the equivalent emf lies between $\\varepsilon_1$ and $\\varepsilon_2$.\n(iii) With like terminals joined and $\\varepsilon_1 = \\varepsilon_2 = \\varepsilon$, the equivalent emf is $\\varepsilon$ even if $r_1 \\neq r_2$.\n(iv) If the negative terminal of the second cell is joined to the positive terminal of the first, then $\\frac{\\varepsilon_{eq}}{r_{eq}} = \\frac{\\varepsilon_1}{r_1} + \\frac{\\varepsilon_2}{r_2}$.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $\\frac{1}{r_{eq}}$ is a sum of positive terms, so it exceeds each $\\frac{1}{r_i}$.\n(ii) Correct: $\\varepsilon_{eq}$ is a weighted average of $\\varepsilon_1$ and $\\varepsilon_2$, with weights $\\frac{1}{r_1}$ and $\\frac{1}{r_2}$.\n(iii) Correct: $\\varepsilon_{eq} = \\frac{\\varepsilon r_2 + \\varepsilon r_1}{r_1 + r_2} = \\varepsilon$.\n(iv) Incorrect: for the reversed cell, $\\varepsilon_2 \\rightarrow -\\varepsilon_2$, so the term becomes $-\\frac{\\varepsilon_2}{r_2}$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":94,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A cell of emf 12 V and internal resistance 0.5 $\\Omega$ is connected across an external resistor of 5.5 $\\Omega$. What is the potential difference across the terminals of the cell?","image":"","options":["12 V","11 V","10 V","1 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\varepsilon$ = 12 V, r = 0.5 $\\Omega$, R = 5.5 $\\Omega$.\nFormula: $I = \\frac{\\varepsilon}{R+r}$ and the terminal voltage is $V = \\varepsilon - Ir$.\nSubstituting: I = 12/6 = 2 A, so V = 12 − 2 × 0.5.\nSo V = 11 V. The value 12 V would be the open-circuit voltage (the emf), and 1 V is only the drop across the internal resistance.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":95,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In a Wheatstone bridge ABCD, a 6 V cell of negligible internal resistance is connected across A and C. A galvanometer of resistance 10 $\\Omega$ is connected across B and D. The arm resistances are AB = 10 $\\Omega$, BC = 20 $\\Omega$, AD = 20 $\\Omega$ and DC = 10 $\\Omega$. What is the magnitude of the current through the galvanometer?","image":"","options":["85.7 mA","0 mA","120 mA","42.9 mA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $V_A = 6$ V, $V_C = 0$. The bridge is unbalanced because $\\frac{10}{20} \\neq \\frac{20}{10}$.\nJunction rule at B: $\\frac{6 - V_B}{10} = \\frac{V_B}{20} + \\frac{V_B - V_D}{10}$, which gives $5V_B - 2V_D = 12$.\nJunction rule at D: $\\frac{6 - V_D}{20} = \\frac{V_D}{10} + \\frac{V_D - V_B}{10}$, which gives $-2V_B + 5V_D = 6$.\nSolving: $V_D = \\frac{54}{21} \\approx 2.571$ V and $V_B \\approx 3.429$ V.\n$I_g = \\frac{V_B - V_D}{10} \\approx 0.0857$ A = 85.7 mA, flowing from B to D.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":96,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Electric current is a scalar quantity, even though it is shown with an arrow in circuit diagrams.\nStatement II: Kirchhoff's junction rule follows from conservation of charge, because no charge accumulates at a junction when currents are steady.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: $I = \\vec{j}\\cdot\\Delta\\vec{S}$ is a scalar product, so currents do not add like vectors. The arrow only shows the sense of flow.\nStatement II is correct: with steady currents, charge does not pile up at any point. So the current flowing in equals the current flowing out.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":97,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A power station supplies 1.1 MW to a town through cables of total resistance 5 $\\Omega$. When the transmission voltage is 11 kV, the power wasted in the cables is 50 kW. What is the power wasted in the same cables if the same 1.1 MW is delivered at 220 kV?","image":"","options":["2500 W","125 W","6.25 W","50000 W"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $P = 1.1\\times10^6$ W, $R_c = 5\\ \\Omega$, $V = 2.2\\times10^5$ V.\nFormula: $P_c = \\frac{P^2R_c}{V^2}$. For fixed P and $R_c$, $P_c \\propto 1/V^2$.\nThe voltage rises 20 times, so the loss falls by a factor of $20^2 = 400$.\nSubstituting: $P_c = \\frac{50000}{400}$ = 125 W. Check: $\\frac{1.21\\times10^{12}\\times5}{4.84\\times10^{10}} = 125$ W.\nOption (1), 2500 W, wrongly assumes the loss scales as $1/V$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":98,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Kirchhoff's junction rule states that at any junction of a circuit, the total current entering equals the total current leaving. This rule is a direct consequence of which of the following?","image":"","options":["Conservation of energy, since potential is a function of position only","Conservation of charge, since in steady state no charge piles up at a junction","Ohm's law, since $V = IR$ holds in every branch","Conservation of momentum of the drifting electrons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"When currents are steady, charge cannot accumulate at a junction or at any point on a wire.\nSo the rate at which charge flows in must equal the rate at which it flows out. This is conservation of charge.\nOption (1) is the tempting wrong choice. Energy conservation, with potential depending only on position, is the basis of the loop rule, not the junction rule.\nOhm's law plays no role, because the junction rule holds even for non-ohmic devices.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":99,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A Wheatstone bridge has arms AB = P, BC = Q, AD = R and DC = S. A cell is connected across AC and a galvanometer across BD. The bridge is balanced ($\\frac{P}{Q} = \\frac{R}{S}$). How many of the following changes, each made separately, leave the bridge balanced?\n(i) The emf of the cell is doubled\n(ii) The galvanometer is replaced by one of higher resistance\n(iii) The cell and the galvanometer are interchanged\n(iv) P and Q are both doubled\n(v) P and S are both doubled","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Balanced: the emf does not appear in the balance condition. \n(ii) Balanced: no current flows through the galvanometer at balance, so its resistance is irrelevant. \n(iii) Balanced: zero current now requires $\\frac{P}{R} = \\frac{Q}{S}$. This is the same as $PS = QR$, which is already true. \n(iv) Balanced: the ratio $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is unchanged. \n(v) Not balanced: $\\frac{P}{Q}$ doubles while $\\frac{R}{S}$ halves. \nFour changes keep the balance. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":100,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In a Wheatstone bridge ABCD, a cell is connected between A and C, and a galvanometer is connected between B and D. The arm resistances are AB = 10 $\\Omega$, BC = 20 $\\Omega$ and AD = 15 $\\Omega$. An unknown resistance X is in arm DC. The galvanometer shows null deflection. What is the value of X?","image":"","options":["30 $\\Omega$","7.5 $\\Omega$","13.3 $\\Omega$","45 $\\Omega$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: AB = 10 $\\Omega$, BC = 20 $\\Omega$, AD = 15 $\\Omega$, DC = X, and the bridge is balanced.\nFormula: at balance, B and D are at the same potential. So the drops from A are equal and $\\frac{AB}{BC} = \\frac{AD}{DC}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{10}{20} = \\frac{15}{X}$, so $X = \\frac{15 \\times 20}{10}$.\nSo X = 30 $\\Omega$.\nOption (2), 7.5 $\\Omega$, comes from inverting the ratio by mistake.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":101,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a metal under a constant electric field, the free electrons move with a constant average (drift) velocity, even though a constant force $-e\\vec{E}$ acts on each of them.\nReason (R): Each electron is accelerated only until its next collision with an ion. Since its post-collision velocity is random, it gains an average extra velocity of only $\\frac{eE\\tau}{m}$, where $\\tau$ is the relaxation time.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: averaging over all electrons gives a time-independent $\\vec{v}_d = -\\frac{e\\vec{E}\\tau}{m}$.\nR is true: each electron gains velocity $-\\frac{e\\vec{E}}{m}t_i$ after its last collision. The random post-collision velocities average to zero, and $t_i$ averages to $\\tau$.\nSo R is exactly the reason why the average velocity stays constant instead of growing.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":102,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Two cells are connected in parallel with their positive terminals joined together. Cell 1 has emf 2.0 V and internal resistance 1 $\\Omega$. Cell 2 has emf 1.5 V and internal resistance 0.5 $\\Omega$. The combination is connected across an external resistor of 3 $\\Omega$. What is the current through the external resistor?","image":"","options":["0.50 A","0.56 A","0.78 A","1.17 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\varepsilon_1$ = 2 V, $r_1$ = 1 $\\Omega$; $\\varepsilon_2$ = 1.5 V, $r_2$ = 0.5 $\\Omega$; R = 3 $\\Omega$.\nFormula: $\\varepsilon_{eq} = \\frac{\\varepsilon_1 r_2 + \\varepsilon_2 r_1}{r_1 + r_2}$, $r_{eq} = \\frac{r_1 r_2}{r_1 + r_2}$, and $I = \\frac{\\varepsilon_{eq}}{R + r_{eq}}$.\nSubstituting: $\\varepsilon_{eq}$ = (1 + 1.5)/1.5 = 5/3 V and $r_{eq}$ = 1/3 $\\Omega$, so I = (5/3)/(10/3).\nSo I = 0.50 A. The option 0.56 A ignores $r_{eq}$, and 0.78 A treats the cells as if they were in series.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":103,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A steady current $I$ flows through a conductor from end A to end B, and the potential difference between them is $V = V(A) - V(B) > 0$. What is the change in potential energy of the charge that passes from A to B in time $\\Delta t$?","image":"","options":["$+IV\\Delta t$","$-IV\\Delta t$","Zero, because the current is steady","$-\\frac{I\\Delta t}{V}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Charge moving in time $\\Delta t$: $\\Delta Q = I\\Delta t$.\nPotential energy change = final minus initial = $\\Delta Q[V(B) - V(A)] = -\\Delta Q\\,V$.\nSo $\\Delta U_{pot} = -IV\\Delta t$. It is negative because the charge moves from higher to lower potential.\nIt is not zero: a steady current means a steady drift, but the charge still loses potential energy.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":104,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The resistivity of a semiconductor decreases as its temperature is raised.\nReason (R): In a semiconductor, the number of free charge carriers per unit volume increases with temperature, and this increase more than compensates for the decrease in relaxation time.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Unlike metals, semiconductors show resistivity falling with temperature (Fig. 3.10).\nR is true. From $\\rho = \\frac{m}{ne^2\\tau}$, $\\tau$ decreases with temperature, but $n$ rises strongly in semiconductors.\nThe rise in $n$ dominates over the fall in $\\tau$, so $\\rho$ falls. R therefore directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":105,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (material) with List-II (property related to resistivity).\nList-I:\n(a) Copper\n(b) Nichrome\n(c) Germanium\n(d) Rubber\nList-II:\n(i) Resistivity decreases as temperature rises\n(ii) Resistivity about $10^{18}$ times that of metals or more\n(iii) Resistivity depends only very weakly on temperature\n(iv) Positive $\\alpha$; $\\rho$–$T$ graph linear near room temperature but curved at very low temperature","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(a) Copper is a metal with positive $\\alpha$, and its graph is non-linear far below $0^\\circ$C (Fig. 3.8), so (iv).\n(b) Nichrome has very weak temperature dependence (Fig. 3.9), so (iii).\n(c) Germanium is a semiconductor, so its resistivity falls with temperature: (i).\n(d) Rubber is an insulator with resistivity $\\sim 10^{18}$ times that of metals or more, so (ii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":106,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Two cells are connected in parallel with like terminals joined together. Cell 1 has emf 6.0 V and internal resistance 2.0 $\\Omega$. Cell 2 has emf 3.0 V and internal resistance 1.0 $\\Omega$. The combination is connected across an external resistor of $\\frac{10}{3}$ $\\Omega$. What is the current through the external resistor?","image":"","options":["1.0 A","1.125 A","2.25 A","0.92 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\varepsilon_1 = 6$ V, $r_1 = 2\\ \\Omega$, $\\varepsilon_2 = 3$ V, $r_2 = 1\\ \\Omega$, $R = \\frac{10}{3}\\ \\Omega$.\nFormula: $\\varepsilon_{eq} = \\frac{\\varepsilon_1 r_2 + \\varepsilon_2 r_1}{r_1 + r_2}$ and $r_{eq} = \\frac{r_1 r_2}{r_1 + r_2}$.\nSubstituting: $\\varepsilon_{eq} = \\frac{6(1) + 3(2)}{3} = 4$ V.\nSimilarly, $r_{eq} = \\frac{2 \\times 1}{3} = \\frac{2}{3}\\ \\Omega$.\nThen $I = \\frac{\\varepsilon_{eq}}{R + r_{eq}} = \\frac{4}{\\frac{10}{3} + \\frac{2}{3}} = \\frac{4}{4}$.\nSo I = 1.0 A. Taking the simple average emf of 4.5 V gives 1.125 A, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":107,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A metal wire is stretched uniformly to twice its original length, keeping its volume constant. The same potential difference is maintained across its ends before and after stretching, and temperature is unchanged.\nAssertion (A): The drift speed of the free electrons in the wire becomes half of its original value.\nReason (R): Drift speed is $v_d = \\frac{eE\\tau}{m}$, and the electric field in the wire is $E = V/l$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: both relations come from NCERT. $\\tau$ depends on the material and temperature, not on the wire's shape.\nWith V fixed and l doubled, E halves, so $v_d$ halves. A is true and follows from R.\nCross-check: R becomes 4 times, so I falls to I/4. Area becomes A/2. Then $v_d = I/(neA)$ scales as $(1/4)/(1/2) = 1/2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":108,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Electrons in a wire drift at only about a millimetre per second. Yet a bulb several metres away lights up almost the instant the switch is closed. Why?","image":"","options":["The electric field is set up throughout the circuit almost instantly, so electrons everywhere start drifting at once","Electrons near the switch travel to the bulb at nearly the speed of light","Closing the switch greatly raises the thermal speed of the electrons","The drift speed becomes very large for a short time just after the switch is closed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"When the circuit is closed, the electric field spreads through the conductor at nearly the speed of light ($\\sim3\\times10^8$ m/s).\nThis field makes the electrons at every point drift locally, including those already inside the bulb's filament.\nSo the current does not wait for electrons to travel from the switch to the bulb.\nOption (2) fails because the electrons themselves only drift slowly.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":109,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The resistivity of a metallic conductor increases with rise in temperature.\nReason (R): In a metal, the number of free electrons per unit volume increases appreciably with rise in temperature.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. For metals $\\alpha$ is positive, so resistivity rises with temperature.\nR is false. In a metal, $n$ does not depend on temperature to any appreciable extent.\nThe real reason is in $\\rho = \\frac{m}{ne^2\\tau}$: as temperature rises, collisions become more frequent, so $\\tau$ falls and $\\rho$ rises.\nAn increase in $n$ would actually lower $\\rho$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":110,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Metals have resistivities roughly in the range $10^{-8}\\,\\Omega\\,m$ to $10^{-6}\\,\\Omega\\,m$.\nStatement II: The resistivity of a semiconductor can be decreased by adding small amounts of suitable impurities.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT gives the resistivity range of metals as $10^{-8}$ to $10^{-6}\\,\\Omega\\,m$.\nStatement II is correct. Adding suitable impurities lowers the resistivity of a semiconductor, and this feature is exploited in electronic devices.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":111,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Electrons in a conductor have a mobility of $4.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m$^2$V$^{-1}$s$^{-1}$. A uniform electric field of 0.50 V/m is applied. What is the drift speed of the electrons?","image":"","options":["8.0 mm/s","0.125 mm/s","2.0 mm/s","1.0 mm/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu = 4.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m$^2$/Vs, E = 0.50 V/m.\nFormula: $\\mu = \\frac{|v_d|}{E}$, so $|v_d| = \\mu E$.\nSubstituting: $|v_d| = 4.0 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 0.50 = 2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m/s.\nSo $v_d$ = 2.0 mm/s. Dividing instead of multiplying gives the wrong options.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":112,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) For a metal, resistivity increases with temperature mainly because the average time between collisions decreases.\n(ii) In metals, the number density of free electrons increases appreciably with temperature.\n(iii) The temperature coefficient of resistivity has the dimension (temperature)$^{-1}$.\n(iv) For metals, the temperature coefficient of resistivity is negative.\n(v) The resistivity of a semiconductor can be decreased by adding small amounts of suitable impurities.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: from $\\rho = \\frac{m}{ne^2\\tau}$, faster electrons collide more often, so $\\tau$ falls and $\\rho$ rises.\n(ii) Incorrect: in metals, $n$ does not depend appreciably on temperature.\n(iii) Correct: since $\\alpha(T-T_0)$ is dimensionless, $\\alpha$ has the dimension (temperature)$^{-1}$.\n(iv) Incorrect: $\\alpha$ is positive for metals.\n(v) Correct: this is the basis of doping in semiconductors.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":113,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Thin discs carrying charges +Q and –Q are attached to the two flat ends of a metallic cylinder.\nAssertion (A): The free electrons in the cylinder move towards the end carrying +Q.\nReason (R): The electric field inside the cylinder points from the +Q end to the –Q end, and a negative charge feels a force opposite to the field.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. The field is directed from the positive charge towards the negative charge, so it points from +Q to –Q. Electrons carry charge –e, so the force on them, –eE, is opposite to the field.\nA is true. Because of this force, the electrons accelerate towards +Q and begin to neutralise it.\nR directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":114,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A cell has emf $\\varepsilon$ and internal resistance r. Match the condition in List-I with the terminal potential difference in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Open circuit (no current)\n(B) Terminals joined by a wire of zero resistance\n(C) Cell delivering current I to an external resistor\n(D) Cell being charged with current I\nList-II\n(P) $\\varepsilon + Ir$\n(Q) Zero\n(R) $\\varepsilon$\n(S) $\\varepsilon - Ir$","image":"","options":["A-R, B-Q, C-S, D-P","A-R, B-S, C-Q, D-P","A-Q, B-R, C-S, D-P","A-R, B-Q, C-P, D-S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) With I = 0, the terminal voltage equals $\\varepsilon$ (R).\n(B) With R = 0, $I_{max} = \\varepsilon/r$, so $V = \\varepsilon - (\\varepsilon/r)r = 0$ (Q).\n(C) During discharge, $V = \\varepsilon - Ir$ (S).\n(D) During charging, current is driven from P to N through the cell, so $V = \\varepsilon + Ir$ (P).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":115,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A Wheatstone bridge is balanced, so the galvanometer shows null deflection. Its balance condition is $\\frac{R_2}{R_1} = \\frac{R_4}{R_3}$. The values are $R_1 = 10\\ \\Omega$, $R_2 = 20\\ \\Omega$ and $R_3 = 15\\ \\Omega$. What is the unknown resistance $R_4$?","image":"","options":["7.5 Ω","15 Ω","30 Ω","20 Ω"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $R_1 = 10\\ \\Omega$, $R_2 = 20\\ \\Omega$, $R_3 = 15\\ \\Omega$, and the bridge is balanced. \nFormula: rearranging the balance condition gives $R_4 = R_3\\frac{R_2}{R_1}$. \nSubstituting: $R_4 = 15 \\times \\frac{20}{10}$. \nSo $R_4 = 30\\ \\Omega$. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":116,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A storage battery of emf 6.0 V and internal resistance 0.5 Ω is charged from a 24 V dc supply. A series resistor is connected in the charging circuit and chosen so that the charging current is exactly 2.0 A. What is the potential drop across this series resistor?","image":"","options":["17 V","18 V","19 V","7 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\varepsilon = 6.0$ V, $r = 0.5\\ \\Omega$, supply = 24 V, $I = 2.0$ A.\nWhile charging, current enters the battery at its positive terminal. So its terminal voltage is $V = \\varepsilon + Ir = 6 + 2 \\times 0.5 = 7$ V.\nThe series resistor takes the rest of the supply voltage: $24 - 7 = 17$ V. This also gives $R = \\frac{17}{2} = 8.5\\ \\Omega$.\n18 V wrongly ignores the internal resistance. 19 V wrongly uses $\\varepsilon - Ir$, which applies only when the battery is discharging.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":117,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"While applying the loop rule, you traverse various elements in a chosen direction. Cells are ideal, and the current I in each resistor is known. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (element traversed)\n(P) Resistor R, traversed along the current I\n(Q) Resistor R, traversed against the current I\n(R) Cell of emf $\\varepsilon$, traversed from negative to positive terminal\n(S) Cell of emf $\\varepsilon$, traversed from positive to negative terminal\nList-II (change in potential)\n(1) $+\\varepsilon$\n(2) $-IR$\n(3) $-\\varepsilon$\n(4) $+IR$","image":"","options":["P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3","P-4, Q-2, R-1, S-3","P-2, Q-4, R-3, S-1","P-4, Q-2, R-3, S-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(P) Current flows from higher to lower potential in a resistor, so moving along the current gives a drop: $-IR$.\n(Q) Moving against the current gives a rise: $+IR$.\n(R) Going from the negative to the positive terminal of an ideal cell gives a rise of $+\\varepsilon$.\n(S) Going the reverse way gives $-\\varepsilon$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":118,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A cell of unknown emf and internal resistance is connected first to a 4 $\\Omega$ resistor, and the current is 0.50 A. It is then connected to a 9 $\\Omega$ resistor, and the current is 0.25 A. What is the maximum current that can be drawn from this cell?","image":"","options":["2.5 A","2.0 A","1.25 A","5.0 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: I = 0.50 A with R = 4 $\\Omega$, and I = 0.25 A with R = 9 $\\Omega$.\nFormula: $\\varepsilon = I(R + r)$ for each case.\nSo $0.5(4 + r) = 0.25(9 + r)$, which gives $2 + 0.5r = 2.25 + 0.25r$, so r = 1 $\\Omega$.\nThen $\\varepsilon = 0.5 \\times 5 = 2.5$ V.\nThe maximum current flows when R = 0: $I_{max} = \\frac{\\varepsilon}{r} = \\frac{2.5}{1}$\nSo $I_{max}$ = 2.5 A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":119,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"An electric bulb is rated 100 W, 230 V. What is the resistance of its filament when it operates at the rated voltage?","image":"","options":["529 $\\Omega$","2.3 $\\Omega$","52900 $\\Omega$","1.89 $\\Omega$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $P = 100$ W, $V = 230$ V.\nFormula: $P = \\frac{V^2}{R}$, so $R = \\frac{V^2}{P}$.\nSubstituting: $R = \\frac{(230)^2}{100} = \\frac{52900}{100}$.\nSo R = 529 $\\Omega$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":120,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In a metal wire, $5.0 \\times 10^{18}$ electrons cross a cross-section in the backward direction in 2.0 s, and no other charge flows. What is the magnitude of the steady current? (Take $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C)","image":"","options":["0.4 A","0.8 A","1.6 A","0.2 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $N = 5.0 \\times 10^{18}$, $t = 2.0$ s, $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C.\nFormula: $I = \\frac{q}{t}$, where $q = Ne$.\nSubstituting: $q = 5.0 \\times 10^{18} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19} = 0.8$ C, so $I = \\frac{0.8}{2.0}$.\nSo the magnitude of the current is 0.4 A. The value 0.8 A is the charge in coulombs, not the current.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":121,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the mobility $\\mu$ of conduction electrons in a metal are correct?\n(i) $\\mu$ is a positive quantity even though electrons drift opposite to $\\vec{E}$.\n(ii) A longer average relaxation time $\\tau$ means a larger mobility.\n(iii) The dimensional formula of mobility is $[M^{-1} L^0 T^2 A]$.\n(iv) The conductivity can be written as $\\sigma = n e \\mu$.\n(v) Mobility of electrons in a metal increases as its temperature rises.\n(vi) 1 cm$^2$/Vs $= 10^4$ m$^2$/Vs.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $\\mu = |v_d|/E$ uses the magnitude of the drift velocity, so it is positive.\n(ii) Correct: $\\mu = e\\tau/m$, so $\\mu$ is proportional to $\\tau$.\n(iii) Correct: this matches NCERT's table.\n(iv) Correct: $\\sigma = ne^2\\tau/m = ne(e\\tau/m) = ne\\mu$.\n(v) Incorrect: collisions become more frequent at higher temperature, so $\\tau$ and hence $\\mu$ decrease.\n(vi) Incorrect: the conversion is reversed; 1 m$^2$/Vs $= 10^4$ cm$^2$/Vs.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":122,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A metal wire is connected across a battery of fixed voltage V. The wire is then stretched uniformly to twice its original length, with its volume and temperature unchanged, and reconnected across the same battery.\nStatement I: The drift speed of the free electrons becomes half of its original value.\nStatement II: The current in the wire becomes half of its original value.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Since the volume is constant, doubling l halves A. Then $R = \\frac{\\rho l}{A}$ becomes 4R, so I becomes $\\frac{I}{4}$. Statement II is incorrect.\nDrift speed: $v_d = \\frac{I}{neA}$ changes by $\\frac{1/4}{1/2} = \\frac{1}{2}$, so it halves.\nCheck: $v_d = \\frac{eE\\tau}{m}$ with $E = \\frac{V}{l}$. Doubling l halves E, so $v_d$ halves. Statement I is correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":123,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform wire has a resistance of 4 $\\Omega$. It is drawn out uniformly so that its length becomes three times the original length, while its volume and material stay the same. What is its new resistance?","image":"","options":["12 $\\Omega$","36 $\\Omega$","$\\frac{4}{3}$ $\\Omega$","108 $\\Omega$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: R = 4 $\\Omega$, l' = 3l, and the volume is constant.\nFormula: $R = \\frac{\\rho l}{A}$. Since the volume $lA$ is fixed, A' = A/3.\nSubstituting: $R' = \\frac{\\rho (3l)}{A/3} = 9\\frac{\\rho l}{A} = 9 \\times 4$.\nSo R' = 36 $\\Omega$. The option 12 $\\Omega$ results from forgetting that the area also decreases.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":124,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Consider a metallic conductor carrying a steady current. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The average kinetic energy of the charge carriers keeps increasing as they move along the conductor.\n(ii) Energy gained by the carriers from the field is shared with the atoms during collisions, so the conductor heats up.\n(iii) The power dissipated in the conductor is $P = IV$.\n(iv) The forms $I^2R$ and $V^2/R$ follow from $P = IV$ using Ohm's law.\n(v) As charge moves from higher to lower potential, its potential energy increases.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: collisions keep the carriers at a steady drift velocity, so they do not keep accelerating.\n(ii) Correct: the atoms vibrate more vigorously, which is Joule heating.\n(iii) Correct: the energy dissipated is $IV\\Delta t$, so $P = IV$.\n(iv) Correct: substitute $V = IR$ into $P = IV$.\n(v) Incorrect: $\\Delta U = -IV\\Delta t$, which is a decrease.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":125,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"The heating element of an electric heater has a resistance of $50\\ \\Omega$ at room temperature ($27^\\circ$C), measured with a negligibly small current. When the heater is connected to a 220 V supply, the current settles to a steady value of 4.0 A. The average temperature coefficient of resistance of the element is $2.0\\times10^{-4}\\ ^\\circ\\text{C}^{-1}$. What is the steady temperature of the element?","image":"","options":["$527^\\circ$C","$482^\\circ$C","$500^\\circ$C","$627^\\circ$C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $R_1 = 50\\ \\Omega$ at $27^\\circ$C, $V = 220$ V, $I = 4.0$ A, $\\alpha = 2.0\\times10^{-4}\\ ^\\circ$C$^{-1}$.\nSteady resistance: $R_2 = \\frac{V}{I} = \\frac{220}{4.0} = 55\\ \\Omega$.\nFormula: $R_2 = R_1[1+\\alpha(T_2-T_1)]$, so $T_2 - T_1 = \\frac{R_2-R_1}{R_1\\alpha} = \\frac{5}{50\\times2\\times10^{-4}} = 500^\\circ$C.\nSo $T_2 = 500 + 27 = 527^\\circ$C.\nOption (3) forgets to add room temperature, and option (2) wrongly uses $R_2$ as the reference.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":126,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A cell of emf 6.0 V and internal resistance 0.5 $\\Omega$ is part of a network. Kirchhoff analysis shows that a current of 2.0 A flows through the cell from its positive terminal P to its negative terminal N, inside the cell. What is the potential difference $V(P) - V(N)$?","image":"","options":["5.0 V","6.0 V","7.0 V","4.0 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\varepsilon$ = 6.0 V, r = 0.5 $\\Omega$, I = 2.0 A flowing from P to N inside the cell.\nFormula: when the current through the cell goes from P to N, $V = \\varepsilon + Ir$. The form $\\varepsilon - Ir$ applies only when the current goes from N to P.\nSubstituting: V = 6.0 + 2.0 × 0.5.\nSo V(P) − V(N) = 7.0 V.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":127,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A copper wire of cross-sectional area $1.5 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$ carries a current of 4.5 A. The resistivity of copper is $1.7 \\times 10^{-8}$ $\\Omega$ m. What is the magnitude of the electric field inside the wire?","image":"","options":["0.051 V/m","0.51 V/m","$5.1 \\times 10^{-3}$ V/m","0.0255 V/m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: I = 4.5 A, $A = 1.5 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$, $\\rho = 1.7 \\times 10^{-8}$ $\\Omega$ m.\nStep 1: $j = \\frac{I}{A} = \\frac{4.5}{1.5 \\times 10^{-6}} = 3.0 \\times 10^6$ A/m$^2$.\nStep 2: Ohm's law in field form is $E = \\rho j$.\nSubstituting: $E = 1.7 \\times 10^{-8} \\times 3.0 \\times 10^6 = 5.1 \\times 10^{-2}$ V/m.\nSo E = 0.051 V/m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":128,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a balanced Wheatstone bridge, the galvanometer arm can be removed or replaced by any resistor without changing the currents in the four arms.\nStatement II: The balance condition of a Wheatstone bridge depends on the resistance of the galvanometer.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. At balance, B and D are at the same potential. Any element between them carries zero current, so removing or replacing it leaves the other currents unchanged. This is how balanced bridges are simplified. \nStatement II is incorrect. The condition $\\frac{R_2}{R_1} = \\frac{R_4}{R_3}$ is obtained with $I_g = 0$, so the galvanometer resistance never enters it. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":129,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: With no applied electric field, the average velocity of the free electrons in a metal is zero, even though each electron moves with a large thermal speed.\nStatement II: With no applied electric field, the free electrons in a metal are at rest, which is why there is no net current.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Electrons keep moving because of thermal motion. After each collision with an ion, an electron's velocity points in a random direction. On average, as many electrons move in any direction as in the opposite direction, so the average velocity is zero.\nStatement II is incorrect. The electrons are not at rest. There is no net current because their random velocities cancel, not because they are stationary.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":130,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Wire-bound standard resistors are often made from alloys such as nichrome, manganin and constantan. What property of these alloys makes them suitable for this use?","image":"","options":["Their resistivity is extremely low, close to that of pure copper","Their resistivity changes very little with temperature","Their resistivity decreases sharply with rise in temperature","Their resistivity can be lowered greatly by adding small impurities"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that nichrome, manganin and constantan show only a very weak dependence of resistivity on temperature.\nA standard resistor must keep the same value when it warms up, so this property is exactly what is needed.\nOption (1) is wrong because these alloys have higher resistivity than pure metals like copper.\nOptions (3) and (4) describe semiconductors, not these alloys.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":131,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform metal wire is connected across a battery that keeps a fixed potential difference V across it. The wire is then removed and drawn out so that its diameter becomes half its original value. Its volume and resistivity stay the same. It is reconnected across the same potential difference V. What is the ratio of the new current density in the wire to the original current density?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{4}$","4","$\\frac{1}{16}$","1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: diameter halved, so $A' = \\frac{A}{4}$. Constant volume then gives $l' = 4l$.\nThe resistance becomes $R' = \\rho \\frac{4l}{A/4} = 16R$, so the current becomes $I' = \\frac{I}{16}$.\nThe new current density is $j' = \\frac{I'}{A'} = \\frac{I/16}{A/4} = \\frac{j}{4}$.\nCheck using $j = \\frac{E}{\\rho} = \\frac{V}{\\rho l}$: the length is 4 times larger, so E and hence j become one quarter.\nOption (3) is the current ratio, not the current density ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":132,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A cell of emf $\\varepsilon$ and internal resistance r delivering current I (from N to P inside it) has terminal voltage $\\varepsilon - Ir$.\n(ii) The largest current a cell could deliver, in principle, is $\\varepsilon/r$, obtained when the external resistance is zero.\n(iii) Two cells in parallel with like terminals joined have equivalent internal resistance $\\frac{r_1r_2}{r_1+r_2}$.\n(iv) Two cells in series with their negative terminals joined have $\\varepsilon_{eq} = \\varepsilon_1 - \\varepsilon_2$ and $r_{eq} = r_1 - r_2$.\n(v) Dry cells have much lower internal resistance than common electrolytic cells.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is Eq. 3.38.\n(ii) Correct: with R = 0, $I_{max} = \\varepsilon/r$, though the safe current is much lower.\n(iii) Correct: $1/r_{eq} = 1/r_1 + 1/r_2$.\n(iv) Incorrect: the emfs subtract, but internal resistances always add, so $r_{eq} = r_1 + r_2$.\n(v) Incorrect: dry cells have much HIGHER internal resistance.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":133,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In an electrolyte, during 4.0 s, a net positive charge of 8.0 C crosses a plane in the forward direction. During the same interval, a net negative charge of 4.0 C (in magnitude) crosses the plane in the backward direction. What is the current across the plane in the forward direction?","image":"","options":["1.0 A","2.0 A","3.0 A","12 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $q_+ = +8.0$ C forward.\nThe 4.0 C of negative charge moves backward, so the net negative charge moving forward is $q_- = -4.0$ C.\nFormula: $q = q_+ - q_-$ and $I = \\frac{q}{t}$.\nSubstituting: $q = 8.0 - (-4.0) = 12$ C, so $I = \\frac{12}{4.0}$.\nSo I = 3.0 A forward. Negative charge moving backward adds to the forward current; subtracting it wrongly gives 1.0 A.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":134,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A battery of emf 9.0 V and internal resistance 1.0 $\\Omega$ is connected to an external resistor of 8.0 $\\Omega$. What is the potential difference across the terminals of the battery?","image":"","options":["9.0 V","1.0 V","10 V","8.0 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $\\varepsilon = 9.0$ V, r = 1.0 $\\Omega$, R = 8.0 $\\Omega$.\nFormula: $I = \\frac{\\varepsilon}{R+r}$, then $V = \\varepsilon - Ir$\nSubstituting: $I = \\frac{9.0}{9.0} = 1.0$ A, so $V = 9.0 - 1.0 \\times 1.0$\nSo the terminal voltage = 8.0 V. This also equals IR = 1.0 × 8.0.\nOption (2) is only the drop across r. Option (3) uses the charging formula $\\varepsilon + Ir$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":135,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a fixed power delivered to a distant town through cables of fixed resistance, doubling the transmission voltage halves the power lost in the cables.\nStatement II: While a storage battery of emf $\\varepsilon$ and internal resistance r is being charged with current I, its terminal voltage is $\\varepsilon + Ir$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The cable loss is $P_c = \\frac{P^2R_c}{V^2}$. Doubling V reduces the loss to one-quarter, not one-half.\nStatement II is correct. During charging, current enters the battery at its positive terminal and flows from P to N inside it. So $V = \\varepsilon + Ir$, as in Eq. (3.60).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":136,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A power station must deliver 2.2 MW to a town through transmission cables of total resistance $5\\ \\Omega$. If the power is transmitted at 220 kV instead of 22 kV, what is the power wasted in the cables?","image":"","options":["0.5 kW","5 kW","50 kW","0.05 kW"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: P = 2.2 × $10^6$ W, $R_c$ = 5 Ω, V = 2.2 × $10^5$ V\nFormula: $P_c = I^2R_c = \\frac{P^2R_c}{V^2}$\nSubstituting: I = P/V = 10 A, so $P_c$ = $10^2$ × 5 = 500 W\nCheck: at 22 kV the current is 100 A and the loss is 50 kW. Raising V by a factor of 10 cuts the loss by a factor of 100.\nSo the power wasted = 0.5 kW\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":137,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Two cells have emfs $\\varepsilon_1$ and $\\varepsilon_2$ ($\\varepsilon_1 > \\varepsilon_2$) and internal resistances $r_1$ and $r_2$. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Equivalent emf, series, current leaving each cell from its positive electrode\n(B) Equivalent emf, series, negative terminals joined\n(C) Equivalent internal resistance, parallel, like terminals joined\n(D) Equivalent emf, parallel, like terminals joined\nList-II\n(I) $\\varepsilon_1 - \\varepsilon_2$\n(II) $\\frac{\\varepsilon_1 r_2 + \\varepsilon_2 r_1}{r_1 + r_2}$\n(III) $\\varepsilon_1 + \\varepsilon_2$\n(IV) $\\frac{r_1 r_2}{r_1 + r_2}$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Cells aiding in series: the emfs add, giving (III).\n(B) Negative terminals joined: the cells oppose, giving $\\varepsilon_1 - \\varepsilon_2$, which is (I).\n(C) Parallel internal resistance follows $\\frac{1}{r_{eq}} = \\frac{1}{r_1} + \\frac{1}{r_2}$, giving (IV).\n(D) Parallel emf is the weighted expression (II). Note it has the dimension of voltage, unlike (IV), which has the dimension of resistance.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":138,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A metal wire has a resistance of $20\\ \\Omega$ at $20^\\circ$C. The temperature coefficient of resistance of the metal is $4.0 \\times 10^{-3}\\ ^\\circ\\text{C}^{-1}$. Ignoring any change in dimensions, what is its resistance at $70^\\circ$C?","image":"","options":["$22\\ \\Omega$","$24\\ \\Omega$","$20.8\\ \\Omega$","$28\\ \\Omega$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $R_0 = 20\\ \\Omega$, $\\alpha = 4.0\\times10^{-3}\\ ^\\circ$C$^{-1}$, $T - T_0 = 50^\\circ$C.\nFormula: $R_T = R_0[1+\\alpha(T-T_0)]$.\nSubstituting: $R_T = 20(1 + 4.0\\times10^{-3}\\times 50) = 20 \\times 1.2$.\nSo $R_T = 24\\ \\Omega$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":139,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"The net charge that has crossed a cross-section of a conductor up to time t is given by $Q(t) = 2t^2 + 3t$, where Q is in coulombs and t is in seconds. What is the current at the instant t = 2 s?","image":"","options":["7 A","11 A","14 A","4 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $Q(t) = 2t^2 + 3t$.\nFormula: the instantaneous current is $I(t) = \\lim_{\\Delta t \\to 0} \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{dQ}{dt}$.\nSubstituting: $I = 4t + 3$, so at t = 2 s, $I = 4(2) + 3$.\nSo I = 11 A. The value 7 A is the average current, $\\frac{Q(2)}{2} = \\frac{14}{2}$, not the instantaneous current.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":140,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Three branches are connected in parallel between two nodes X (top) and Y (bottom).\nBranch 1: a 10 V cell in series with a total resistance of 2 $\\Omega$.\nBranch 2: a 4 V cell in series with a total resistance of 1 $\\Omega$.\nBranch 3: a 4 $\\Omega$ resistor.\nBoth cells have their positive terminals towards X. Take the current in branch 2 as positive when it leaves the positive terminal of the 4 V cell. What is the current in branch 2?","image":"","options":["$-1.14$ A","$+1.14$ A","$+2.43$ A","$+1.29$ A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let V = V(X) − V(Y). Assume all currents flow towards X.\nJunction rule at X: $\\frac{10-V}{2} + \\frac{4-V}{1} = \\frac{V}{4}$.\nMultiplying by 4: 36 = 7V, so V = 36/7 V ≈ 5.14 V.\nBranch 2 current = (4 − 36/7)/1 = −8/7 ≈ −1.14 A. The 4 V cell is therefore being charged.\nCheck: branch 1 carries 17/7 A, and 17/7 − 8/7 = 9/7 A, which is the 4 $\\Omega$ current.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":141,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In a metal, the rise in resistivity with temperature is mainly due to a decrease in relaxation time $\\tau$, while n stays nearly constant.\n(ii) In a semiconductor, resistivity falls with temperature because $\\tau$ increases with temperature.\n(iii) Conduction electrons drift opposite to the electric field, i.e., towards higher potential.\n(iv) Inside a conductor carrying current, electron paths between successive collisions are straight lines.\n(v) Mobility equals $\\frac{e\\tau}{m}$ for electrons.","image":"","options":["3","2","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: τ decreases because collisions become more frequent, and n hardly changes, so $\\rho = \\frac{m}{ne^2\\tau}$ increases.\n(ii) Incorrect: in a semiconductor n increases with temperature. This more than compensates the decrease in τ.\n(iii) Correct: electrons carry charge –e, so they drift against E, which means towards increasing potential.\n(iv) Incorrect: when a field is present, the paths between collisions are, in general, curved.\n(v) Correct: $\\mu = \\frac{|v_d|}{E} = \\frac{e\\tau}{m}$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":142,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Twelve identical resistors, each of 2 Ω, form the edges of a cube. What is the equivalent resistance between two diagonally opposite corners of the cube?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{5}{3}$ Ω","2.5 Ω","1.2 Ω","$\\frac{5}{6}$ Ω"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: each edge R = 2 Ω; the battery is across a body diagonal.\nBy symmetry, the current splits as I, I/2, I. Applying the loop rule along a path from one corner to the opposite corner gives ε = IR + (I/2)R + IR = (5/2)IR.\nThe total current is 3I, so Req = ε/(3I) = (5/6)R.\nSubstituting: Req = (5/6) × 2 = 5/3 Ω ≈ 1.67 Ω.\nThe value 5/6 Ω is correct only when R = 1 Ω.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":143,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match each type of V–I behaviour in List-I with the example in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) V is not proportional to I (the curve bends away from a straight line)\n(b) The current for a given |V| depends on the sign of V\n(c) More than one value of V for the same current I\n(d) Linear V–I graph passing through the origin\nList-II\n(p) GaAs\n(q) Metallic wire kept at constant temperature\n(r) Diode\n(s) Good conductor at large currents (resistivity rising with I)","image":"","options":["a-s, b-r, c-p, d-q","a-r, b-s, c-p, d-q","a-s, b-p, c-r, d-q","a-q, b-r, c-p, d-s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(s): Fig. 3.5 shows a good conductor whose V–I curve deviates from the linear Ohm's law line, for example when ρ increases with I.\n(b)-(r): A diode passes very different currents for +V and –V of the same magnitude.\n(c)-(p): GaAs has a non-unique V–I relation, so one current can correspond to several voltages.\n(d)-(q): A metallic wire at constant temperature obeys Ohm's law, giving a straight line through the origin.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":144,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Using $\\rho = \\frac{m}{ne^2\\tau}$ and related ideas, consider the following statements:\n(i) In a metal, resistivity rises with temperature mainly because the average collision time $\\tau$ decreases.\n(ii) In a semiconductor, $\\tau$ increases with temperature, and this is why its resistivity decreases.\n(iii) For copper, the graph of $\\rho$ versus $T$ remains a straight line even at temperatures far below $0\\,^\\circ C$.\n(iv) The relation $\\rho_T = \\rho_0[1+\\alpha(T-T_0)]$ can be used approximately over a limited range around any reference temperature $T_0$.\n(v) In insulators, the number of free charge carriers per unit volume increases with temperature.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: in a metal $n$ is nearly constant, so the fall in $\\tau$ makes $\\rho$ rise.\n(ii) Incorrect: $\\tau$ also decreases in a semiconductor; the rise in $n$ more than compensates for it.\n(iii) Incorrect: the graph deviates considerably from a straight line well below $0\\,^\\circ C$.\n(iv) Correct: the linear relation holds approximately over a limited range around any $T_0$.\n(v) Correct: $n$ increases with temperature for insulators and semiconductors.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":145,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When two identical conducting slabs, each of resistance R, are placed end to end so that the same current passes through both, the resistance of the combination is 2R.\nStatement II: When the length of a copper wire is doubled, the resistivity of copper also doubles.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The same current I flows through both slabs. The potential differences add to give 2V, so $R_C = 2V/I = 2R$.\nStatement II is incorrect. Resistivity depends only on the material and on conditions such as temperature. It does not depend on the dimensions of the wire.\nDoubling the length doubles the resistance R, not the resistivity ρ.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":146,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a neutral metallic wire carrying a steady current, the total charge density is zero, yet the current density is not zero.\nReason (R): The relation $j = \\rho v$ must be applied separately to each type of charge carrier. In a metal the positive ions are nearly stationary, so $j \\approx \\rho_- v_-$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the wire is neutral, so $\\rho_+ = -\\rho_-$ and the total $\\rho = \\rho_+ + \\rho_- = 0$. Yet a current clearly flows.\nR is true: the total current density is $j = \\rho_+ v_+ + \\rho_- v_-$. With $v_+ \\approx 0$, this gives $j = \\rho_- v_- \\neq 0$.\nSo R explains why j is non-zero even though the total charge density is zero.\nThis is why $j = \\rho v$ cannot be applied to the total charge density.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":147,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A current of 2 A flows through a resistor of resistance 20 $\\Omega$. What power is dissipated as heat in the resistor?","image":"","options":["40 W","10 W","80 W","160 W"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $I = 2$ A, $R = 20\\ \\Omega$.\nFormula: $P = I^2R$.\nSubstituting: $P = (2)^2 \\times 20 = 4 \\times 20$.\nSo P = 80 W.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":148,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform metal wire is connected across a battery that keeps a fixed potential difference across it (ignore temperature changes). The wire is now stretched uniformly to twice its original length, with its volume unchanged, and reconnected to the same potential difference. Which option correctly describes the new drift speed of electrons and the new current?","image":"","options":["Drift speed becomes half and current becomes one-fourth","Drift speed doubles and current becomes half","Drift speed is unchanged and current becomes one-fourth","Drift speed becomes half and current becomes half"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The field inside the wire is $E = \\frac{V}{l}$. Doubling l at fixed V halves E.\nSince $v_d = \\frac{eE\\tau}{m}$ and n, $\\tau$ are unchanged for the same material, $v_d$ halves. The cross-section does not appear in this formula.\nWith constant volume, doubling the length halves the area A.\nUsing $I = neAv_d$, the current becomes $\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}$ of its original value.\nCheck: $R = \\frac{\\rho l}{A}$ becomes 4R, so I becomes one-fourth, which agrees.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":149,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A steady current flows in a copper wire. Which of the following statements about the conduction electrons is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Their drift velocity is opposite in direction to the electric field","They drift from the region of lower potential towards higher potential","All the free electrons move in the same direction, that of the drift","Without a field, their paths between successive collisions are straight lines"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: $\\vec{v}_d = -\\frac{e\\vec{E}\\tau}{m}$, so the drift is opposite to $\\vec{E}$.\n(2) Correct: $\\vec{E}$ points towards lower potential, so electrons drift towards higher potential.\n(3) Incorrect: the small drift is superposed on large random velocities, so the electrons still move in all directions.\n(4) Correct: with no force acting between collisions, each path is a straight line.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":150,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"In a platinum resistance thermometer, the resistance of the platinum wire is $4.00\\,\\Omega$ at the ice point and $4.40\\,\\Omega$ at the steam point. When the thermometer is placed in a hot liquid, the resistance becomes $5.10\\,\\Omega$. Assuming a linear variation of resistance with temperature, what is the temperature of the liquid?","image":"","options":["$127.5\\,^\\circ C$","$225\\,^\\circ C$","$275\\,^\\circ C$","$300\\,^\\circ C$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $R_0 = 4.00\\,\\Omega$, $R_{100} = 4.40\\,\\Omega$, $R_t = 5.10\\,\\Omega$.\nFormula: from $R_t = R_0(1+\\alpha t)$, we get $t = \\frac{R_t - R_0}{R_{100}-R_0}\\times 100$.\nSubstituting: $t = \\frac{1.10}{0.40}\\times 100$\nSo $t = 275\\,^\\circ C$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":151,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If the net charge crossing an area in the forward direction, divided by time, turns out to be a negative number, the current is actually in the backward direction.\nStatement II: The SI unit of current, the ampere, is defined through the magnetic effects of currents.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. $I = \\frac{q}{t}$ is defined in the chosen forward direction. A negative value simply means the current flows backward.\nStatement II: Correct. NCERT states that the ampere is defined through the magnetic effects of currents.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":152,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"At a junction in a circuit carrying steady currents, currents of 3 A and 2 A flow in through two wires. One outgoing wire carries 1.5 A. Only one other wire is connected to the junction. What is the current in it, and is it flowing in or out?","image":"","options":["0.5 A, entering","1.5 A, leaving","6.5 A, leaving","3.5 A, leaving"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: incoming currents are 3 A and 2 A; one outgoing current is 1.5 A.\nFormula (junction rule): sum of currents in = sum of currents out.\nSubstituting: 3 + 2 = 1.5 + I, so I = 3.5 A.\nI is positive on the 'out' side, so this current leaves the junction.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":153,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A metallic cylinder has discs carrying +Q and –Q fixed to its two flat ends.\nCase P: the charges on the discs are not replenished.\nCase S: a mechanism continuously supplies fresh charge to the discs to replace any charge that is neutralised.\nWhich option correctly describes how the current in the cylinder varies with time in the two cases?","image":"","options":["P: current flows briefly and then falls to zero; S: current settles to a steady non-zero value","P: current stays constant indefinitely; S: current keeps increasing because the electrons are always being accelerated","P: current is zero at all times because the metal shields the charges; S: current settles to a steady non-zero value","P: current flows briefly and then falls to zero; S: current increases linearly with time because the field never disappears"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Case P: the moving electrons neutralise +Q and –Q. Once the charges are gone, the field disappears, so the current lasts only a short while and then becomes zero.\nCase S: replenishing the charges keeps a steady field inside the conductor. Collisions with ions stop the electrons from speeding up indefinitely, so they settle to a constant drift velocity. This gives a steady current, not a growing one.\nOption (4) ignores collisions. Option (3) ignores the brief current that does flow in Case P.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":154,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the currents that flow in the nerves of our body are typically of the order of:","image":"","options":["tens of thousands of amperes","one ampere","microamperes","kiloamperes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT gives three typical magnitudes of current.\nDomestic appliances carry currents of the order of 1 A.\nAn average lightning stroke carries tens of thousands of amperes.\nNerve currents lie at the other extreme and are only microamperes.\nThe tempting option, one ampere, is the scale of household appliances, not nerves.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":155,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II. The quantities refer to a copper wire carrying a few amperes at room temperature, with order-of-magnitude values.\nList-I\n(A) Drift speed of conduction electrons\n(B) Thermal speed of copper atoms\n(C) Speed at which the electric field travels along the wire\n(D) Number density of free electrons\nList-II\n(I) $\\sim 3\\times10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$\n(II) $\\sim 1$ mm s$^{-1}$\n(III) $\\sim 10^{29}$ m$^{-3}$\n(IV) $\\sim 2\\times10^{2}$ m s$^{-1}$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The drift speed is about 1 mm/s for currents of a few amperes, so A-II.\n(B) The thermal speed of copper atoms at 300 K, of order $\\sqrt{k_BT/M}$, is about $2\\times10^2$ m/s, so B-IV.\n(C) The field travels at the speed of an electromagnetic wave, $3\\times10^8$ m/s, so C-I.\n(D) The free electron density in a metal is about $10^{29}$ m$^{-3}$, so D-III. This huge density is why a tiny drift still gives a large current.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":156,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A junction diode is a non-ohmic device.\nReason (R): For a diode, reversing the applied voltage while keeping its magnitude fixed does not produce a current of the same magnitude in the opposite direction.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. A diode does not follow a linear V-I relation that is the same for both polarities.\nR is true. NCERT gives the diode as its example of a V-I relation that depends on the sign of V. The forward and reverse currents differ greatly for the same |V|.\nThis sign dependence is exactly why the diode violates Ohm's law, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":157,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Heat dissipated in a conductor in time $\\Delta t$\n(B) Power dissipated in a resistor, given its current and resistance\n(C) Power wasted in transmission cables, given the delivered power and the transmission voltage\n(D) Change in potential energy of the charge flowing through a conductor in time $\\Delta t$\nList-II\n(i) $-IV\\Delta t$\n(ii) $\\frac{P^2R_c}{V^2}$\n(iii) $IV\\Delta t$\n(iv) $I^2R$","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A)-(iii): energy dissipated as heat is $\\Delta W = IV\\Delta t$, a positive quantity.\n(B)-(iv): substituting $V = IR$ into $P = IV$ gives $P = I^2R$.\n(C)-(ii): $P_c = I^2R_c$ with $I = P/V$ gives $\\frac{P^2R_c}{V^2}$.\n(D)-(i): the potential energy decreases, so $\\Delta U = -IV\\Delta t$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":158,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"While applying Kirchhoff's rules, a cell of emf 6 V and internal resistance 0.5 Ω is found to carry a current of 2 A. This current flows inside the cell from its positive terminal P to its negative terminal N, so the cell is being charged. What is the potential difference $V(P) - V(N)$?","image":"","options":["5 V","6 V","7 V","8 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: emf ε = 6 V, r = 0.5 Ω, I = 2 A flowing from P to N inside the cell.\nFormula: when the current goes from P to N through the cell, V = ε + Ir. (V = ε − Ir applies only when the current flows from N to P.)\nSubstituting: V = 6 + 2 × 0.5 = 7 V.\nSo V(P) − V(N) = 7 V.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":159,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Electrical power from generating stations is sent over long distances at very high voltages.\nReason (R): For a given power delivered to a load, the power wasted in the transmission cables is inversely proportional to the square of the transmission voltage.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Power delivered is $P = VI$, so the current is $I = P/V$.\nCable loss: $P_c = I^2R_c = \\frac{P^2R_c}{V^2}$, which varies as $1/V^2$. So R is true.\nCables are hundreds of miles long and $R_c$ is considerable, so loss is cut by using enormous V. So A is true.\nR directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":160,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A bulb glows almost immediately after the switch is closed because free electrons travel from the switch to the bulb at nearly the speed of light.\nReason (R): When a circuit is closed, the electric field is set up throughout the circuit almost instantly, causing a local drift of electrons at every point.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false. Drift speeds are only about a millimetre per second, so electrons do not race from the switch to the bulb.\nR is true. The field spreads through the circuit at nearly the speed of light. Electrons everywhere, including those in the filament, start drifting at once.\nThis is why the bulb glows almost immediately, even though A gives the wrong reason.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":161,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a switch is closed, current is established in a circuit almost instantly, even though the drift speed of electrons is only about a millimetre per second.\nReason (R): Free electrons leave the battery and travel through the wire at nearly the speed of light.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: current starts almost immediately when the circuit is closed.\nR is false: electrons only drift, at about $10^{-3}$ m/s. What travels at nearly the speed of light is the electric field, which is set up throughout the circuit almost instantly.\nThis field causes local drift at every point at once. No electron needs to travel from one end of the wire to the other.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":162,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (physical quantity) with List-II (SI unit):\nList-I\n(P) Mobility\n(Q) Resistivity\n(R) Current density\n(S) Relaxation time\nList-II\n(1) $\\Omega$ m\n(2) s\n(3) $m^2 V^{-1} s^{-1}$\n(4) A $m^{-2}$","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2","P-1, Q-3, R-4, S-2","P-3, Q-1, R-2, S-4","P-4, Q-1, R-3, S-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(P) Mobility $\\mu = v_d/E$ has unit $(m/s)/(V/m) = m^2V^{-1}s^{-1}$, so P-3.\n(Q) Resistivity comes from $R = \\rho l/A$, giving $\\Omega$ m, so Q-1.\n(R) Current density is current per unit area, A $m^{-2}$, so R-4.\n(S) Relaxation time is an average time between collisions, measured in s, so S-2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":163,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (physical quantity) with List-II (SI unit).\nList-I:\n(a) Resistance\n(b) Resistivity\n(c) Conductivity\n(d) Current density\nList-II:\n(i) $\\Omega^{-1}$ m$^{-1}$\n(ii) A m$^{-2}$\n(iii) V A$^{-1}$\n(iv) $\\Omega$ m","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Resistance: $R = \\frac{V}{I}$, so its unit is 1 $\\Omega$ = 1 V A$^{-1}$, which is (iii).\n(b) Resistivity: $\\rho = \\frac{RA}{l}$, so its unit is $\\Omega$ m, which is (iv).\n(c) Conductivity: $\\sigma = \\frac{1}{\\rho}$, so its unit is $\\Omega^{-1}$ m$^{-1}$ (S m$^{-1}$), which is (i).\n(d) Current density: $j = \\frac{I}{A}$, so its unit is A m$^{-2}$, which is (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":164,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about a balanced Wheatstone bridge is correct?","image":"","options":["The current through the galvanometer is maximum at balance","The currents in all four resistors are necessarily equal","Balance is possible only when all four resistances are equal","The two ends of the galvanometer arm are at the same potential"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: at balance the galvanometer current is zero, not maximum. \n(2) Incorrect: only the resistors that are in series carry the same current. These are the pairs $I_1 = I_3$ and $I_2 = I_4$. The two branches can carry different currents. \n(3) Incorrect: only the ratios of the resistances must match. \n(4) Correct: zero current through the galvanometer means there is no potential difference across it. \nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":165,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A cell of emf 3 V and internal resistance 1 $\\Omega$ is connected in parallel with a cell of emf 1 V and internal resistance 1 $\\Omega$. The positive terminal of the first cell is joined to the negative terminal of the second, and the remaining two terminals are joined together. What is the potential difference across the combination when no external load is connected?","image":"","options":["1.0 V","2.0 V","4.0 V","1.5 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\varepsilon_1$ = 3 V, $\\varepsilon_2$ = 1 V, and $r_1 = r_2$ = 1 $\\Omega$. The second cell is reversed.\nFormula: $\\varepsilon_{eq} = \\frac{\\varepsilon_1 r_2 - \\varepsilon_2 r_1}{r_1 + r_2}$, using the rule $\\varepsilon_2 \\rightarrow -\\varepsilon_2$.\nSubstituting: $\\varepsilon_{eq} = \\frac{3 \\times 1 - 1 \\times 1}{2}$ = 1 V.\nWith no load, the open-circuit terminal voltage equals $\\varepsilon_{eq}$. So V = 1.0 V. Taking the cells as aiding would wrongly give 2.0 V.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":166,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A bulb glows almost the instant the switch is closed, even though electrons drift through the wire at only a few mm/s.\nReason (R): On closing the circuit, the electric field is set up throughout the conductor at nearly the speed of light, so electrons everywhere in the circuit begin drifting at once.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Current is established almost instantly in practice.\nR is true. The field spreads through the circuit at nearly the speed of light and causes a local electron drift at every point.\nR explains A because current does not have to wait for electrons from the switch to reach the bulb. Electrons already present in the filament start drifting immediately.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":167,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A student intends to connect 10 identical cells in series, each of emf 1.5 V and internal resistance 0.5 $\\Omega$. By mistake, 2 of the cells are connected with reversed polarity. The battery is connected across an external resistor of 10 $\\Omega$. What is the current in the circuit?","image":"","options":["0.8 A","1.0 A","0.64 A","0.6 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: 10 cells (1.5 V, 0.5 $\\Omega$ each), 2 of them reversed, R = 10 $\\Omega$.\nEmf: 8 cells aid and 2 oppose, so $\\varepsilon_{eq}$ = (8 − 2) × 1.5 = 9 V. Each reversed cell cancels one aiding cell as well as removing its own contribution.\nResistance: all internal resistances still add, so $r_{eq}$ = 10 × 0.5 = 5 $\\Omega$.\nCurrent: I = 9/(10 + 5) = 0.6 A. Merely dropping the 2 reversed cells would give 12/15 = 0.8 A.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":168,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about a balanced Wheatstone bridge are correct?\n(i) The balance condition does not depend on the emf of the cell in the battery arm.\n(ii) The two resistors joined in series on the same side of the galvanometer arm carry equal currents.\n(iii) The bridge becomes unbalanced if the galvanometer is replaced by one of higher resistance.\n(iv) If the cell and the galvanometer are interchanged, the bridge remains balanced.\n(v) The meter bridge works on the principle of the Wheatstone bridge.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the condition $\\frac{R_2}{R_1} = \\frac{R_4}{R_3}$ contains only resistances.\n(ii) Correct: with $I_g = 0$, the junction rule at B and D makes the series currents equal.\n(iii) Incorrect: there is no potential difference across the galvanometer arm, so its resistance does not matter.\n(iv) Correct: the interchanged condition is an algebraic rearrangement of the same ratio.\n(v) Correct: NCERT states that the meter bridge uses this principle.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":169,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"The temperature of a copper wire is raised by a moderate amount. How many of the following quantities increase?\n(i) Resistivity of the wire\n(ii) Relaxation time $\\tau$ of free electrons\n(iii) Number density n of free electrons\n(iv) Resistance of the wire\n(v) Conductivity of the wire","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Increases: $\\rho = \\frac{m}{ne^2\\tau}$ and $\\tau$ falls.\n(ii) Decreases: electrons move faster, so collisions become more frequent.\n(iii) Practically unchanged: in a metal, n does not depend appreciably on temperature.\n(iv) Increases, because $\\rho$ increases.\n(v) Decreases, since $\\sigma = 1/\\rho$.\nOnly (i) and (iv) increase, so the count is 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":170,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the temperature dependence of resistivity is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In a metal, the number density of free electrons hardly changes with temperature, but the relaxation time decreases, so resistivity increases with temperature","In a semiconductor, the increase in carrier density with temperature outweighs the fall in relaxation time, so resistivity decreases","Nichrome is used in standard resistors because its resistivity varies strongly and linearly with temperature","The resistivity–temperature graph of copper departs considerably from a straight line at temperatures far below 0 °C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: $\\rho = m/(ne^2\\tau)$. In a metal, n is nearly constant while $\\tau$ falls as T rises, so $\\rho$ rises.\n(2) Correct: in a semiconductor, the rise in n dominates the fall in $\\tau$, so $\\rho$ falls.\n(3) Incorrect: nichrome shows a very WEAK temperature dependence. That is exactly why it, along with manganin and constantan, is used in standard resistors.\n(4) Correct: Fig. 3.8 shows this deviation at low temperatures.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":171,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"The heating element of an appliance has a resistance of $80\\,\\Omega$ at room temperature ($27\\,^\\circ C$), measured with a negligibly small current. When the element is run on a $220\\,V$ supply, it reaches a steady state in which it consumes $550\\,W$. The temperature coefficient of resistance of the element, averaged over the temperature range involved, is $2.0\\times10^{-4}\\,^\\circ C^{-1}$. What is the steady temperature of the element?","image":"","options":["$473\\,^\\circ C$","$500\\,^\\circ C$","$527\\,^\\circ C$","$627\\,^\\circ C$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $R_1 = 80\\,\\Omega$ at $27\\,^\\circ C$, $V = 220\\,V$, $P = 550\\,W$, $\\alpha = 2.0\\times10^{-4}\\,^\\circ C^{-1}$.\nFirst find the hot resistance: $R_2 = \\frac{V^2}{P} = \\frac{48400}{550} = 88\\,\\Omega$.\nFormula: $T_2 - T_1 = \\frac{R_2 - R_1}{R_1\\,\\alpha}$\nSubstituting: $T_2 - T_1 = \\frac{8}{80\\times2.0\\times10^{-4}} = 500\\,^\\circ C$\nSo $T_2 = 500 + 27 = 527\\,^\\circ C$. Option (2) gives only the temperature rise, not the final temperature.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":172,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about a cell of emf $\\varepsilon$ and internal resistance r are correct?\n(i) The emf of the cell decreases when the external resistance connected to it is decreased.\n(ii) With no current flowing, the electrolyte is at the same potential throughout.\n(iii) While the cell supplies current to an external resistor, current inside the cell flows from the positive electrode to the negative electrode.\n(iv) The internal resistance may be neglected in calculations when $\\varepsilon \\gg Ir$.\n(v) A series resistor is used in a charging circuit to limit the current drawn from the supply.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: $\\varepsilon = V_+ + V_-$ depends on the cell itself. Only the terminal voltage $\\varepsilon - Ir$ changes with R.\n(ii) Correct: this is why the open-circuit P–N difference equals $V_+ + V_-$.\n(iii) Incorrect: inside the electrolyte, current flows from N to P. Through the external resistor it flows from P to N.\n(iv) Correct: when $\\varepsilon \\gg Ir$, the drop Ir is negligible.\n(v) Correct: the series resistor limits the charging current.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":173,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"A storage battery of emf 12 V and internal resistance 0.5 $\\Omega$ is being charged from a 24 V dc supply. A series resistor of 5.5 $\\Omega$ is included in the circuit. What is the terminal voltage of the battery during charging?","image":"","options":["11 V","13 V","12 V","24 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: supply 24 V, $\\varepsilon = 12$ V, r = 0.5 $\\Omega$, series resistor 5.5 $\\Omega$.\nDuring charging, the battery's emf opposes the supply.\nSo $I = \\frac{24 - 12}{5.5 + 0.5} = 2$ A.\nWhile charging, current enters the battery at its positive terminal, so $V = \\varepsilon + Ir$.\nSubstituting: $V = 12 + 2 \\times 0.5 = 13$ V.\nThe series resistor limits the charging current.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":174,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"The mobility of electrons in a sample is $4.5 \\times 10^{-3}$ m$^2$V$^{-1}$s$^{-1}$. What is this mobility in the practical unit cm$^2$/Vs?","image":"","options":["0.45 cm$^2$/Vs","45 cm$^2$/Vs","450 cm$^2$/Vs","$4.5 \\times 10^{-7}$ cm$^2$/Vs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu = 4.5 \\times 10^{-3}$ m$^2$/Vs.\nConversion: 1 m$^2$ = $10^4$ cm$^2$, so 1 m$^2$/Vs = $10^4$ cm$^2$/Vs.\nSubstituting: $\\mu = 4.5 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 10^4$ cm$^2$/Vs.\nSo $\\mu$ = 45 cm$^2$/Vs. Dividing by $10^4$ instead of multiplying gives the wrong value $4.5 \\times 10^{-7}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-175","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":175,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a balanced Wheatstone bridge, the galvanometer continues to show zero deflection even if it is replaced by a galvanometer of different resistance.\nReason (R): At balance, the two ends of the galvanometer arm are at the same potential, so no current flows through that arm whatever its resistance.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The balance condition involves only the four arm resistances. It does not involve the galvanometer resistance, so A is true.\nAt balance, B and D are at equal potential. With zero potential difference across the galvanometer arm, the current through it is zero for any resistance, so R is true.\nR directly explains why A holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-176","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":176,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following can move and contribute to an electric current when an electric field is applied?\n(i) Free electrons in a copper wire\n(ii) Positive ions in the ionosphere\n(iii) Negative ions in an electrolytic solution\n(iv) Electrons in rubber, which remain bound\n(v) Positive ions in a solid metal lattice","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: free electrons in metals carry the current.\n(ii) Yes: the ionosphere is an ionised gas, so its free charged particles, including positive ions, can move.\n(iii) Yes: in an electrolyte, both positive and negative ions move.\n(iv) No: bound electrons do not accelerate even when a field is applied.\n(v) No: ions in a solid lattice are tightly bound and stay fixed.\nSo three of them can carry current.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CURRENT-177","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Current Electricity","qno":177,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Current Electricity","type":"mcq","question":"Two cells, of emf 6 V (internal resistance 1.5 $\\Omega$) and 4 V (internal resistance 0.5 $\\Omega$), are connected in series with their negative terminals joined together. The combination is used between the two free terminals. What are the equivalent emf and the equivalent internal resistance?","image":"","options":["10 V, 2 $\\Omega$","2 V, 1 $\\Omega$","2 V, 2 $\\Omega$","2 V, 0.375 $\\Omega$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Joining the two negative terminals makes the cells oppose each other. The current leaves one cell from its negative electrode, so that cell's emf enters with a minus sign: $\\varepsilon_{eq}$ = 6 − 4 = 2 V.\n(ii) The internal resistances are still in series, so they simply add: $r_{eq}$ = 1.5 + 0.5 = 2 $\\Omega$.\n(iii) The value 0.375 $\\Omega$ is the parallel value and does not apply here.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 3 · Current Electricity","difficulty":"easy"}]});
